\section{Higher-order Lump Waves} %Multi-Lump \label{sec:perperturb}
To derive the higher-order lump wave solutions of the considered GSSDNW model~\eqref{eq1}, we incorporate technical input from the previous reports given in \cite{guo2021multiple, peng2021different}. By imposing the constraint conditions, we wish to eliminate the contribution of the mixed Hirota derivative \( D_{\xi}D_{\tau} \) and the fourth-order Hirota derivative \( D_{\xi}^4 \), thereby simplifying the underlying model. 
Taking into account the constraint condition $\chi_2 =\chi_5 = 0$, the following restrictions have to be imposed: 
 \[
\beta_1 = 2\beta_2\mu^2, \quad \gamma_1 = -\beta_1, \quad \gamma_2 = -\beta_2.
\]
To construct higher-order lump wave solutions, we take a generalized polynomial function for $f(\xi, \tau)$ by following Refs. \cite{clarkson2017rational, zhaqilao2018symbolic, guo2021multiple} as given below.
\begin{equation}\label{eq6}
f(\xi, \tau) = F_{n+1}(\xi, \tau) + 2 \phi \, \xi\, P_n(\xi, \tau) + 2 \psi \, \tau\, Q_n(\xi, \tau) + (\phi^2 + \psi^2)\, F_{n-1}(\xi, \tau),
\end{equation}
where
\[
F_n(\xi, \tau) = \sum_{k=0}^{n(n+1)/2} \sum_{i=0}^{k} a_{n(n+1)-2k,\, 2i}\, \xi^{2i}\, \tau^{n(n+1)-2k},
\]
\[
P_n(\xi, \tau) = \sum_{k=0}^{n(n+1)/2} \sum_{i=0}^{k} b_{n(n+1)-2k,\, 2i}\, \, \tau^{2i}\, \xi^{n(n+1)-2k},
\]
\[
Q_n(\xi, \tau) = \sum_{k=0}^{n(n+1)/2} \sum_{i=0}^{k} c_{n(n+1)-2k,\, 2i}\, \xi^{2i}\, \tau^{n(n+1)-2k}. 
\]
The application of the above polynomial in the bilinear form and solving the resulting equation, one can arrive at the solution in (1+1)-dimensional version, from which the required solution for the (3+1)D GSSDNW equation can be constructed by using the dimensional-expansion.

As an example, to construct the second-order lump solution, we choose $a_{(n+1)(n+2),\,0} = 1$,  $F_0 = 1$, and for $n = 1$, which terminates the polynomial expansion for $f$ as below.
\[
f(\xi, \tau) = F_2(\xi, \tau) + 2 \phi \, \xi\, P_1(\xi, \tau) + 2 \psi \, \tau\, Q_1(\xi, \tau) + (\phi^2 + \psi^2).
\]
The substitution of $F_2(\xi, \tau)$, $P_1(\xi, \tau)$, and $Q_1(\xi, \tau)$, gives the following detailed form for the test function $f(\xi, \tau)$ corresponding to the second-order lump wave: 
\[
\begin{aligned}\label{eq3}
f(\xi, \tau) =\;& (a_{0,0} + a_{0, 2} \xi^2 + a_{0, 4} \xi^4 + a_{0, 6} \xi^6) + (a_{2, 0} + a_{2, 2} \xi^2 + a_{2, 4} \xi^4) \tau^2 + (a_{4, 0} + a_{4, 2} \xi^2) \tau^4 + \tau^6 \\
&+ 2\phi \xi (b_{0, 0} + b_{0, 2} \xi^2 + b_{2, 0} \tau^2) + 2\psi \tau (c_{0, 0} + c_{0, 2} \xi^2 + c_{2, 0} \tau^2) + (\phi^2 + \psi^2) \, .
\end{aligned}
\]
Using the above test function~\eqref{eq3} for second-order lump waves in the bilinear equation~\eqref{eq2.11}, and collecting all terms that involve powers of $\left( \tau^{i} \xi^{j} \right)$, where $\left( i, j = 0, 1, 2, \dots \right)$, we obtain a system of nonlinear algebraic equations. Upon solving this system, we derive the following lump wave parameters corresponding to the second-order lump wave:
\begin{align*}
a_{0,0} &= \frac{-16875\, \chi_1^3 + \chi_3^2 \left( \phi^2\, (-9\, \chi_3 + b_{2,0}^2\, \chi_4) + 9\, ( c_{2,0}^2-1)\, \chi_3\, \psi^2 \right)}{9\, \chi_3^3} \, ; \quad 
a_{0,2} = \frac{475\, \chi_1^2}{\chi_3\, \chi_4} \, ; \\
a_{0,4} &= -\frac{17\, \chi_1\, \chi_3}{\chi_4^2} \, ; \quad 
a_{0,6} =\left( \frac{\chi_3}{\chi_4}\right)^3 \, ; \quad 
a_{2,0} = -125 \left(\frac{\, \chi_1}{\chi_3}\right)^2 \, ; \quad 
a_{2,2} = -\frac{90\, \chi_1}{\chi_4} \, ; \quad 
a_{2,4} = 3\left(\frac{\, \chi_3}{\chi_4}\right)^2 \, ; \\
a_{4,0} &= -\frac{25\, \chi_1}{\chi_3} \, ; \quad 
a_{4,2} = \frac{3\, \chi_3}{\chi_4} \, ; \quad 
b_{0,0} = -\frac{5\, b_{2,0}\, \chi_1}{3\, \chi_3} \, ; \quad 
b_{0,2} = -\frac{b_{2,0}\, \chi_3}{3\, \chi_4} \, ; \\
c_{0,0} &= \frac{c_{2,0}\, \chi_1}{\chi_3} \, ; \quad 
c_{0,2} = -\frac{3\, c_{2,0}\, \chi_3}{\chi_4} \,.
\end{align*}
The above explicit form of parameters and the final expression $f(\xi, \tau)$ gives the required second-order lump-wave solution of the GSSDNW equation (\ref{eq1}) from the bilinear transformations and mapping to the original (3+1)-dimensions. 
This second-order solution contains four arbitrary parameters, namely, $b_{2, 0}, c_{2, 0}, \phi$, and $\psi$ apart from the arbitrary model parameters, and using them all, one can modify the nature and evolution of the lump-wave dynamics effectively. 
For an easy understanding, one such evolution of the second-order lump waves is demonstrated in Fig. \ref{fig:combined2}. It is clear that the second-order lumps exhibit three sets of paired peaks and do not interact during the time evolution. Such a structure resembles the identities of three independent first-order lump-waves, and they combine to form a triangular geometry. As mentioned earlier, the characteristics of the triangular lump waves, like inter- and intra-peak distance, peak amplitude, width of peaks, angle or orientation of the peaks in addition to the orientation of the triangular structure itself, can be tuned by properly adjusting the available arbitrary parameters. 

