With the addition of nine transits, we also conduct a search for transit timing variations (TTVs) that could be caused by a non-transiting companion. Using the \texttt{Batman} \citep{batman} package, we model each individual TESS transit holding the period, $a/R_*$, and $i$ constant at the values found in \S\ref{sec:photmod} while fitting for the transit midpoint and \rprs. We use \texttt{emcee} \citep{emcee} to sample the posterior distribution and then compare the individual transit times with those expected from a linear ephemeris. Our results are shown in Figure \ref{fig:ttv}. We do not observe any significant TTVs, with the standard deviation being comparable to the photometric exposure time (2 minutes).

