\title[On function fields of curves over higher local fields]{On 
function fields of curves over higher local fields and their division 
LFD-algebras}

\keywords{Central division algebra, locally finite-dimensional
algebra, normally locally finite algebra, transcendental
extension, Diophantine dimension, $m$-local
field, iterated Laurent formal power series field\\
2020 MSC Classification: primary 16K40, 12J10, 12F20; secondary
11S15, 16K20.}


\author{Ivan D. Chipchakov}
\address{Institute of Mathematics and Informatics\\Bulgarian
Academy of Sciences\\Acad. G. Bonchev Str., Bl. 8\\1113 Sofia,
Bulgaria:\\ E-mail: chipchak@math.bas.bg}

\begin{abstract}
Let $K _{m}$ be an $m$-local field with an $m$-th residue field $K
_{0}$, for some integer $m > 0$, and let $K/K _{m}$ be a field
extension of transcendence degree trd$(K/K _{m}) \le 1$. This paper 
shows that if $K _{0}$ is a field of finite Diophantine dimension 
(for example, a finitely-generated extension of a finite or a 
pseudo-algebraically closed perfect field $E$), then the absolute 
Brauer $p$-dimension abrd$_{p}(K)$ of $K$ is finite, for every prime 
number $p$. Thus it turns out that if $R$ is an associative locally 
finite-dimensional (abbr., LFD) central division $K$-algebra, then it 
is a normally locally finite algebra over $K$, that is, every 
nonempty finite subset $Y$ of $R$ is contained in a 
finite-dimensional central $K$-subalgebra $\mathcal{R}_{Y}$ of $R$.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle
\par
\medskip
\section{\bf Introduction and statement of the main result}

\medskip
Let $F$ be a field, $A$ an (associative) unital algebra, $A ^{\ast
}$ its multiplicative group, and $Z(A)$ the centre of $A$. By a
subalgebra of $A$, we mean a subalgebra containing the unit of
$A$. We say that $A$ is a locally finite-dimensional (abbr., LFD)
algebra if its finite subsets generate finite-dimensional
$F$-subalgebras; $A$ is called normally locally finite (abbr.,
NLF), if every finite subset $Y \subset A$ is included in a
finite-dimensional central $F$-subalgebra $\Theta _{A}$ of $A$.
The field $F$ is said to be virtually perfect if char$(F) = 0$ or
char$(F) = q > 0$ and the degree $[F\colon F ^{q}]$ is finite,
where $F ^{q} = \{\lambda ^{q}\colon \lambda \in F\}$.
\par
Clearly, NLF-algebras over $F$ are LFD and central $F$-algebras.
The research into infinite-dimensional division NLF-algebras,
initiated by K\"{o}the, has led him to the question of whether
central division LFD-algebras over $F$ are NLF (see
\cite[page~27]{Ko}). It has been shown in \cite{Ch7} that the
answer to this question is, generally, negative. The purpose of
this paper is to prove the normality of central division
LFD-algebras in the following situation:
\par
\medskip
\begin{theo}
\label{theo1.1} Let $K$ be a field and $K _{m}$ an $m$-local field
with a virtually perfect $m$-th residue field $K _{0}$, for some
integer $m > 0$. Then central division {\rm LFD}-algebras over $K$
are {\rm NLF} in the following two cases:
\par
{\rm (a)} {\rm ddim}$(K)$ is finite; this holds if $K$ is a
finitely-generated extension of a finite or a perfect
pseudo-algebraically closed (abbr., {\rm PAC}) field.
\par
{\rm (b)} $K/K _{m}$ is a field extension, {\rm trd}$(K/K _{m})
\le 1$, and {\rm ddim}$(K _{0}) < \infty $.
\end{theo}
\par
\medskip
By a $1$-local field, we mean a complete discrete valued field,
and when $m \ge 2$, an $m$-local field with an $m$-th residue
field $K _{0}$ means a complete field $K _{m}$ relative to a
discrete valuation $w _{0}$, such that the residue field $K
_{m-1}$ of $(K _{m}, w _{0})$ is an $(m - 1)$-local field with an
$(m - 1)$-th residue field $K _{0}$.
\par
A field $F$ is said to be of type $C _{m}$ (or a $C _{m}$-field),
for an integer $m \ge 0$, if every $F$-form $f$ (a homogeneous
polynomial $f \neq 0$ with coefficients in $F$) of degree deg$(f)$
in more than deg$(f) ^{m}$ variables has a nontrivial zero over
$F$. The Diophantine dimension ddim$(F)$ of $F$ is defined as
follows: ddim$(F)$ equals the least integer $m \ge 0$ for which
$F$ is a $C _{m}$-field; ddim$(F)$ is infinity if $F$ is not a $C
_{m'}$-field, for any $m'$. For example, $F$ is a $C _{0}$-field
if and only if it is algebraically closed; also, finite fields
have type $C _{1}$, by the Chevalley-Warning theorem, and PAC
perfect fields have type $C _{2}$ (see
\cite[Proposition~21.2.4]{FJ}, and \cite[Theorem~21.3.6 and
Remark~21.3.7]{FJ}, respectively). Clearly, if ddim$(F) \le m$ and
char$(F) = q > 0$, then $[F\colon F ^{q}] \le q ^{m}$ (the
$F$-form $\sum _{i=1} ^{q^{m'}} a _{i}X _{i} ^{q}$ is without a
nontrivial zero over $F$ if $[F\colon F ^{q}] = q ^{m'}$ and $a
_{i} \in F\colon i = 1, \dots , q ^{m'}$, is an $F ^{q}$-basis of
$F$). The class of $C _{m}$-fields is closed under taking
algebraic extensions; when $m \ge 3$ and $F _{0}$ is a perfect
PAC-field, this class contains all extensions of $F _{0}$ of
transcendence degree $m - 2$, and the iterated Laurent (formal
power) series fields $F _{0}((T _{1})) \dots ((T _{m-2}))$ in $m -
2$ variables over $F _{0}$ (cf. \cite{Na} and \cite{Gr}).
\par
\medskip
To prove Theorem \ref{theo1.1} we need the main result of the
following section. This result is a $p$-primary form of the
Harbater-Hartmann-Krashen and Lieblich theorem (abbr., the
HHKL-theorem), see \cite[Corollaries~5.7, 5.8]{HHKr} and
\cite[Corollary~1.3]{Li11}, which extends the scope of its
applicability to every $m$-local field with an $m$-th residue
field of finite Diophantine dimension.
\par
\smallskip
\section{\bf A $p$-primary form of the
Harbater-Hartmann-Krashen-Lieblich theorem for function fields of
curves over $m$-local fields}
\par
\medskip
Let $F$ be a field, $\mathbb{P}$ the set of prime numbers,
$\mathbb{P}_{F} = \{p \in \mathbb{P}\colon p \neq {\rm
char}(F)\}$, Br$(F)$ the Brauer group of $F$, $s(F)$ the class of
associative finite-dimensional central simple $F$-algebras, and
$d(F)$ the subclass of the division algebras lying in $s(F)$. It
is well-known (cf. \cite[Sects. 12.5 and 14.4]{P}) that Br$(F)$ is
an abelian torsion group, whence, it decomposes into the direct
sum $\oplus _{p \in \mathbb{P}} {\rm Br}(F) _{p}$ of its
$p$-components Br$(F) _{p}$. For each $\nabla \in s(F)$, denote by
ind$(\nabla )$ the Schur index of $\nabla $, that is, the degree
deg$(D _{\nabla })$ of the underlying (central) division
$F$-algebra $D _{\nabla }$ of $\nabla $, determined by
Wedderburn's structure theorem (see \cite[Sect. 3.5]{P}); also,
let exp$(\nabla )$ be the exponent of $\nabla $, i.e. the order of
its (Brauer) equivalence class $[\nabla ]$ as an element of
Br$(F)$. As shown by Brauer (cf. \cite[Sect. 14.4]{P}),
exp$(\nabla )$ divides ind$(\nabla )$, and the sets of prime
divisors of ind$(\Delta )$ and exp$(\nabla )$ coincide. The proof
of these relations allows to obtain the above-noted properties of
Br$(F)$ and to deduce Brauer's primary tensor product
decomposition theorem for every $D \in d(F)$ (cf. \cite[Sects.
13.4, 14.4]{P}). The description of index-exponent pairs over $F$
depends on the Brauer $p$-dimensions Brd$_{p}(F)$, $p \in
\mathbb{P}$ (in the sense of \cite[Sect.~4]{Ch7}), defined for
each $p \in \mathbb{P}$, as follows: Brd$_{p}(F) = n(p)$ if $n(p)$
is the least integer $\ge 0$ for which ind$(A _{p}) \mid {\rm
exp}(A _{p}) ^{n(p)}$ whenever $A _{p} \in s(F)$ and $[A _{p}] \in
{\rm Br}(F) _{p}$; when such $n(p)$ does not exist, we put
Brd$_{p}(F) = \infty $.
\par
The absolute Brauer $p$-dimension abrd$_{p}(E)$\footnote{The
Brauer $p$-dimension, in the sense of \cite{PS}, means the same as
the absolute Brauer $p$-dimension in the present paper.} of $E$,
where $p \in \mathbb{P}$, is defined to be the supremum of
Brd$_{p}(E ^{\prime })$, when $E ^{\prime }$ runs across the set of
finite extensions $E ^{\prime }$ of $E$ in its separable closure $E
_{\rm sep}$. Denote by $\Phi _{\rm Br}$ the class of Brauer
finite-dimensional fields, namely, those fields $\Psi $ for
which there are integers $m _{p}(\Psi )\colon p \in \mathbb{P}$, with
$m _{p}(\Psi ) \ge {\rm Brd}_{p}(\Psi ^{\prime })$, for
every finite extension $\Psi ^{\prime }/\Psi $ and any $p \in
\mathbb{P}$; for brevity, the fields from this class are also
called $\Phi _{\rm Br}$-fields. Clearly, if $E$ is a $\Phi _{\rm
Br}$-field, then abrd$_{p}(E) \le m _{p}(E)$, for every $p \in
\mathbb{P}$. The following lemma shows that abrd$_{p}(E) = m
_{p}(E)$ whenever $p \neq {\rm char}(E)$.
\par
\medskip
\begin{lemm}
\label{lemm3.7} Let $E$ be a field of characteristic $q > 0$,
$\overline E$ an algebraic closure of $E$, and $E _{\rm ins}$ the
maximal purely inseparable extension of $E$ in $\overline E$.
Then:
\par
{\rm (a)} For each $p \in \mathbb{P}_{E}$, {\rm abrd}$_{p}(E) \ge
{\rm Brd}_{p}(E ^{\prime })$, for every finite extension $E
^{\prime }$ of $E$ in $\overline E$; in addition, {\rm
abrd}$_{p}(E _{\rm ins}) = {\rm abrd}_{p}(E)$;
\par
{\rm (b)} If $E$ is virtually perfect and $[E\colon E ^{q}] = q
^{\delta }$, then {\rm Brd}$_{q}(E ^{\prime }) \le \delta $, for
every finite extension $E ^{\prime }/E$;
\par
{\rm (c)} $E _{\rm ins}$ is a Brauer finite-dimensional field,
provided that so is $E$; the converse holds if $E$ is virtually
perfect.
\end{lemm}
\par
\smallskip
\begin{proof}
It is known that if $E ^{\prime }$ is a finite extension of $E$ in
$\overline E$, then $E ^{\prime } _{\rm ins}$ equals the
compositum $E ^{\prime }.E _{\rm ins}$ and $E ^{\prime }/E _{0}
^{\prime }$ is a purely inseparable extension, where \par\noindent
$E _{0} ^{\prime } = E ^{\prime } \cap E _{\rm sep}$; in
particular, $[E ^{\prime }\colon E _{0} ^{\prime }] = q ^{\nu }$,
for some integer $\nu \ge 0$ (cf. \cite[Ch.~V, Proposition~6.6 and
Corollary~6.2]{L}). Therefore, it follows from Albert-Hochschild's
theorem (cf. \cite[Ch. II, 2.2]{S1}) and \cite[Sect.~13.4,
Proposition~(vi)]{P}, that the scalar extension map of $s(E _{0}
^{\prime })$ into $s(E ^{\prime })$ induces an index-preserving
group isomorphism Br$(E _{0} ^{\prime }) _{p} \cong {\rm Br}(E
^{\prime }) _{p}$, for each \par\noindent $p \in \mathbb{P}_{E}$.
As finite extensions of $E ^{\prime }$ in $E ^{\prime } _{\rm
ins}$ are purely inseparable, whence, their degrees are
$q$-powers, the scalar extension map $s(E ^{\prime }) \to s(E
^{\prime } _{\rm ins})$ induces an index-preserving isomorphism
Br$(E ^{\prime }) _{p} \cong {\rm Br}(E ^{\prime } _{\rm ins})
_{p}$ as well. These facts, and the one that each finite extension
of $E _{\rm ins}$ in $\overline E$ equals $\widetilde E _{\rm
ins}$, for a suitably chosen finite extension $\widetilde E$ of
$E$ in $E _{\rm sep}$ (cf. \cite[Ch.~V, Corollary~6.10]{L}), prove
Lemma \ref{lemm3.7} (a). The former part of Lemma \ref{lemm3.7}
(c) is implied by Lemma \ref{lemm3.7} (a), since every $Y \in
I(\overline E/E _{\rm ins})$ is a perfect field, which ensures
that Br$(Y) _{q} = \{0\}$ (see \cite[Ch. VII, Theorem~22]{A1}); as
to the latter one, it follows from Lemma \ref{lemm3.7} (a)-(b). It
remains for us to prove Lemma \ref{lemm3.7} (b), assuming that
$[E\colon E ^{q}] = q ^{\delta }$, for some integer $\delta > 0$.
Clearly, $E _{\rm ins}$ equals the union $\cup _{n=1} ^{\infty } E
_{q^{-n}}$, where $E _{q^{-n}} = \{\lambda _{n} \in \overline
E\colon \lambda _{n} ^{q^{n}} \in E\}$, for each $n$; also, it is
easily verified that $E _{q^{-n}}/E$ is a field extension, $[E
_{q^{-n}}\colon E] = q ^{\delta n}$, and $E _{q^{-n}} \subset E
_{q^{-n-1}}$. At the same time, by \cite[Ch. VII, Theorem~28]{A1},
$E _{q^{-n-1}}$ is a splitting field of any $D _{n} \in d(E
_{q^{-n}})$ with exp$(D _{n}) = q$. This implies $E _{q^{-n}}$ is
a splitting field of any $D _{n} ^{\prime } \in d(E)$ with exp$(D
_{n} ^{\prime }) = q ^{n}$, which yields deg$(D _{n} ^{\prime })
\mid q ^{\delta n}$ (cf. \cite[Sect.~13.4]{P}), proving that
Brd$_{q}(E) \le \delta $. Since $[E ^{\prime }\colon E ^{\prime
q}] = q ^{\delta }$, for all finite extensions $E ^{\prime }/E$
(this equality is known and can be deduced from
\cite[Lemma~2.7.3]{FJ} and \cite[Ch.~VI, Corollary~9.2]{L}), one
obtains similarly that Brd$_{q}(E ^{\prime }) \le \delta $, as
required by Lemma \ref{lemm3.7} (b).
\end{proof}
\par
\medskip
Before stating the main result of this section, note that the
HHKL-theorem generalizes Saltman's theorem \cite{Salt} on
index-exponent relations in central division algebras over
function fields of $\ell $-adic (algebraic) curves to the respective
algebras over function fields of curves defined over an arbitrary
higher local field with a finite or algebraically closed last residue
field. The generalized Artin conjecture predicts that the
HHKL-theorem holds for every function field of a curve defined over a
higher local field whose last residue field is of finite Diophantine
dimension (see Remark \ref{rema4.4} and the end of Section 4). The
$p$-primary version of the  theorem, presented below, applies to the
considered fields independently of this conjecture, which allows to
prove the normality of their central division LFD-algebras.
\par
\medskip
\begin{theo}
\label{theo2.1} Let $K _{m}$ be an $m$-local field with an $m$-th
residue field $K _{0}$, for some $m \in \mathbb{N}$, and let {\rm
char}$(K _{0}) = q$, and $\mathcal{K}_{m}/K _{m}$,
$\mathcal{K}_{0}/K _{0}$ be purely transcendental extensions with
{\rm trd}$(\mathcal{K}_{i}/K _{i}) = 1$, for $i = 0$ and $i = m$.
Suppose that $\mathcal{K}_{0} \in \Phi _{\rm Br}$, put
$\mathbb{P}_{q} = \mathbb{P}_{K_{0}}$, and for each $p \in
\mathbb{P}$, denote by $b _{p}(K _{0})$ the maximum {\rm
max}$\{{\rm abrd}_{p}(\mathcal{K}_{0}), 1 + {\rm abrd}_{p}(K
_{0})\}$ if abrd$_{p}(\mathcal{K}_{0})
> 0$, and put $b _{p}(K _{0}) = 0$ in case
abrd$_{p}(\mathcal{K}_{0}) = 0$. Then $\mathcal{K}_{m}$ is a
virtually perfect $\Phi _{\rm Br}$-field with {\rm
abrd}$_{p}(\mathcal{K}_{m}) \le b _{p}(K _{0}) + m$, for each $p
\in \mathbb{P}_{q}$; hence, every central division {\rm
LFD}-algebra over an arbitrary finite extension $\mathcal{K}_{m}
^{\prime }$ of $\mathcal{K}_{m}$ is {\rm NLF}.
\end{theo}
\par
\medskip
The latter assertion of Theorem \ref{theo2.1} follows from the
former one, and the normality of central division {\rm
LFD}-algebras over any $\Phi _{\rm Br}$-field (see
\cite[Sect.~3]{Ch7}). The former assertion of Theorem
\ref{theo2.1} is proved in Section 4; this is done relying on the
lemmas presented in Section 3.
\par
\medskip
Note here that Theorem \ref{theo1.1} (b) is implied by Theorems
\ref{theo1.1} (a), \ref{theo2.1} and the Lang-Nagata-Tsen theorem.
As to our proof of Theorem \ref{theo1.1} (a), it is based on the
closeness of the class of $C _{m}$-fields under taking algebraic
extensions, for each $m$, and on Matzri's inequalities Brd$_{p}(E
_{m}) \le p ^{m-1} - 1$, for every $C _{m}$-field $E$ and each $p \in
\mathbb{P} _{q}$  (cf. \cite{Mat}). Since $C _{m}$-fields are
virtually perfect, these inequalities and Lemma \ref{lemm3.7}
show that they are Brauer finite-dimensional, which proves the former
part of Theorem \ref{theo1.1} (a). The latter part of Theorem
\ref{theo1.1} (a) follows from the former one, combined with the
facts that ddim$(E _{0}) = 1$ if $E _{0}$ is a finite field, ddim$(E
_{0}) \le 2$ in case $E _{0}$ is a perfect PAC-field, and by the
Lang-Nagata-Tsen theorem (cf. \cite{Na}), ddim$(\mathcal{F}) < \infty
$, for any field extension $\mathcal{F}/F$ with trd$(\mathcal{F}/F) <
\infty $ and ddim$(F) < \infty $.
\par
\medskip
The basic notation, terminology and conventions kept in this paper
are standard and essentially the same as in \cite{TW}, \cite{L},
\cite{P} and \cite{S1}. Brauer groups and ordered abelian groups
are written additively, Galois groups are viewed as profinite with
respect to the Krull topology, and by a profinite group
homomorphism, we mean a continuous one. As usual, $\mathbb{N}$
denotes the set of positive integers, and $\mathbb{Z}$ is the
additive group of integers. Given a field extension $E ^{\prime
}/E$, we write I$(E ^{\prime }/E)$ for the set of intermediate
fields of $E ^{\prime }/E$. When $E ^{\prime }/E$ is Galois,
$\mathcal{G}(E ^{\prime }/E)$ denotes its Galois group, and
$\mathcal{G}_{E}$ stands for the absolute Galois group of $E$,
i.e. $\mathcal{G}_{E} = \mathcal{G}(E _{\rm sep}/E)$. If
$\mathcal{F}/F$ is a purely transcendental field extension with
trd$(\mathcal{F}/F) = 1$ and abrd$_{p}(F)$ finite, for some $p \in
\mathbb{P}$, we put $b _{p}(F) = {\rm max}\{{\rm
abrd}_{p}(\mathcal{F}), 1 + {\rm abrd}_{p}(F)\}$ if
abrd$_{p}(\mathcal{F}) > 0$, and $b _{p}(F) = 0$ in case
abrd$_{p}(\mathcal{F}) = 0$.
\par
\medskip
\section{\bf Some lemmas on the absolute Brauer $p$-dimensions}
\par
\medskip
As noted above, the lemmas included in this section play a major
role in our proof of Theorem \ref{theo2.1}.
\par
\medskip
\begin{lemm}
\label{lemm3.8} Let $E$ be a field, $p$ a prime number, $G _{p}$ a
Sylow pro-$p$ subgroup of $\mathcal{G}_{E}$, $E _{p}$ the fixed
field of $G _{p}$, and let $\mathcal{E}$ be an extension of $E$ in
$E _{\rm sep}$, such that $p \nmid [\widetilde{E} _{0}\colon E]$,
for any $\widetilde{E}_{0} \in I(\widetilde{E}/E)$ of finite
degree $[\widetilde{E}_{0}\colon E]$. Then {\rm Brd}$_{p}(E) \le
{\rm Brd}_{p}(\mathcal{E})$ and {\rm abrd}$_{p}(E) = {\rm
abrd}_{p}(\mathcal{E}) = {\rm abrd}_{p}(E _{p})$.
\end{lemm}
\par
\smallskip
\begin{proof}
For each $E ^{\prime } \in I(E _{\rm sep}/E)$, denote by $\Lambda
_{E'}$ the set of all $E _{1} ^{\prime } \in I(E _{\rm sep}/E
^{\prime })$ with the property that $p$ does not divide the degree
of any finite extension of $E ^{\prime }$ in $E _{1} ^{\prime }$.
Clearly, $E ^{\prime } \in \Lambda _{E'}$, and partially ordered
by set-theoretic inclusion, $\Lambda _{E'}$ satisfies the
conditions of Zorn's lemma; therefore, $\Lambda _{E'}$ contains a
maximal element. Note also that $\Lambda _{E''} = I(E _{\rm sep}/E
^{\prime \prime }) \cap \Lambda _{E'}$, for every $E ^{\prime
\prime } \in \Lambda _{E'}$. This follows from the fact that, for
each $\beta \in E _{\rm sep}$, there is a finite extension $E
^{\prime } _{\beta }$ of $E ^{\prime }$ in $E ^{\prime \prime }$,
such that $[E ^{\prime } _{\beta }(\beta )\colon E ^{\prime }
_{\beta }] = [E ^{\prime \prime }(\beta )\colon E ^{\prime \prime
}]$; one may take as $E ^{\prime } _{\beta }$ the extension of $E
^{\prime }$ generated by the coefficients of the minimal (monic)
polynomial of $\beta $ over $E ^{\prime \prime }$. These
observations allow to deduce from Galois theory and (the version
of) Sylow's theorems for profinite groups (cf. \cite[Ch. I,
1.4]{S1}) that a field $\mathcal{E} ^{\prime } \in I(E _{\rm
sep}/E ^{\prime })$ is a maximal element in $\Lambda _{E'}$ if and
only if $\mathcal{G}_{\mathcal{E}'}$ is a Sylow pro-$p$ subgroup
of $\mathcal{G}_{E'}$. Since $\mathcal{E} \in \Lambda _{E}$, it is
now easy to see that a closed subgroup $\mathcal{H} _{p}$ of
$\mathcal{G}_{\mathcal{E}}$ is a Sylow pro-$p$ subgroup of
$\mathcal{G}_{E}$ if and only if $\mathcal{H}_{p}$ is a Sylow
pro-$p$ subgroup of $\mathcal{G}_{\mathcal{E}}$. Thus it turns out
that Lemma \ref{lemm3.8} will be proved if we show that
Brd$_{p}(E) \le {\rm Brd}_{p}(\mathcal{E})$ and abrd$_{p}(E) =
{\rm abrd}_{p}(E _{p})$. The former inequality follows from the
assumption on $\mathcal{E}$, which ensures that the scalar
extension map $s(E) \to s(\mathcal{E})$ induces an
index-preserving injective group homomorphism Br$(E) _{p} \cong
{\rm Br}(\mathcal{E}) _{p}$.
\par
We turn to the proof of the equality abrd$_{p}(E) = {\rm
abrd}_{p}(E _{p})$. It is not difficult to see that, for each pair
$Y \in I(E _{\rm sep}/E)$, $\Delta _{Y} \in d(Y)$, there exists a
pair $Y _{0} \in I(Y/E)$, $\Delta _{Y _{0}} \in d(Y _{0})$, such
that $[Y _{0}\colon E] < \infty $, exp$(\Delta _{Y _{0}}) = {\rm
exp}(\Delta _{Y})$, and there is an $Y$-isomorphism $\Delta _{Y
_{0}} \otimes _{Y _{0}} Y \cong \Delta _{Y}$ (cf.
\cite[(1.3)]{Ch2}); in particular, deg$(\Delta _{Y _{0}}) = {\rm
deg}(\Delta _{Y})$. This, applied to the case where $Y$ is an
arbitrary finite extension of $E _{p}$ and deg$(\Delta _{Y})$ is a
$p$-power, leads to the conclusion that Brd$_{p}(Y) \le {\rm
abrd}_{p}(E)$ and abrd$_{p}(E _{p}) \le {\rm abrd}_{p}(E)$. For
the proof of the inequality abrd$_{p}(E) \le {\rm abrd}_{p}(E
_{p})$, it is sufficient to show that given a finite extension $Z$
of $E$ in $E _{\rm sep}$, and an algebra $D \in d(Z)$ of $p$-power
degree, there is a finite extension $Z _{p}$ of $E _{p}$ in $E
_{\rm sep}$, such that exp$(D _{p}) = {\rm exp}(D)$ and deg$(D
_{p}) = {\rm deg}(D)$, for some $D _{p} \in d(Z _{p})$. Denote by
$M$ the Galois closure of $Z$ in $E _{\rm sep}$ over $E$, fix a
Sylow $p$-subgroup $H _{p}$ of $\mathcal{G}(M/Z)$, choose a Sylow
$p$-subgroup $G _{p}$ of $\mathcal{G}(M/E)$ so that $H _{p} \le G
_{p}$, and let $Z ^{\prime }$ and $U$ be the fixed fields of $H
_{p}$ and $G _{p}$, respectively. It follows from Galois theory
and the choice of $H _{p}$ and $G _{p}$ that $Z ^{\prime } = Z.U$,
$p$ does not divide $[Z ^{\prime }\colon Z][U\colon E]$, and $[Z
^{\prime }\colon Z]$ equals the index of $H _{p}$ in $G _{p}$
(i.e. the maximal $p$-power dividing $[Z\colon E]$). This implies
$d(Z ^{\prime })$ contains the $Z ^{\prime }$-algebra $D ^{\prime
} = D \otimes _{Z} Z ^{\prime }$, and yields exp$(D ^{\prime }) =
{\rm exp}(D)$ and deg$(D ^{\prime }) = {\rm deg}(D)$.
\par
Let now $\widetilde G _{p}$ be a Sylow pro-$p$ subgroup of
$\mathcal{G}_{U}$, $U _{p}$ the fixed field of $\widetilde G
_{p}$, and $Z ^{\prime } _{p} = Z ^{\prime }.U _{p}$. Then
$\widetilde G _{p}$ is a Sylow pro-$p$ subgroup of
$\mathcal{G}_{E}$, and by Sylow's theorems for profinite groups,
the groups $G _{p}$ and $\widetilde G _{p}$ are conjugate in
$\mathcal{G}_{E}$; hence, by Galois theory, there exists $\tau
_{p} \in \mathcal{G}_{E}$, which induces an isomorphism $U _{p}
\cong E _{p}$ as $E$-algebras. Observing now that each finite
extension $\nabla ^{\prime }$ of $Z ^{\prime }$ in $Z ^{\prime }
_{p}$ is a subfield of the compositum $Z ^{\prime }\nabla $, for
some finite extension $\nabla $ of $U$ in $U _{p}$, and we have
$[\nabla Z ^{\prime }\colon Z ^{\prime }] = [\nabla U\colon U]$,
one obtains that $p \nmid [\nabla ^{\prime }\colon Z ^{\prime }]$.
This implies the $Z _{p} ^{\prime }$-algebra $D _{p} ^{\prime } =
D ^{\prime } \otimes _{Z'} Z _{p} ^{\prime }$ lies in $d(Z _{p}
^{\prime })$, exp$(D _{p} ^{\prime }) = {\rm exp}(D)$ and deg$(D
_{p} ^{\prime }) = {\rm deg}(D)$. At the same time, it becomes
clear that $Z _{p} ^{\prime }$ is an extension of $U _{p}$ with
$[Z _{p} ^{\prime }\colon U _{p}] = [Z ^{\prime }\colon U]$, $Z
_{p} ^{\prime }$ is the fixed field of the group $\widetilde H
_{p} = \widetilde G _{p} \cap \mathcal{G}_{Z'}$, and $\widetilde H
_{p}$ is a Sylow pro-$p$ subgroup of $\mathcal{G}_{Z'}$. Finally,
one concludes that if $Z _{p}$ is the image of $Z _{p} ^{\prime }$
under $\tau _{p}$, then $Z _{p}/E _{p}$ is a field extension, $[Z
_{p}\colon E _{p}] = [Z _{p} ^{\prime }\colon U _{p}] = [Z
^{\prime }\colon U]$, and $\tau _{p}$ gives rise to an
index-preserving isomorphism $\bar \tau _{p}: {\rm Br}(Z _{p}
^{\prime }) _{p} \cong {\rm Br}(Z _{p}) _{p}$. Therefore, if $D
_{p} \in d(Z _{p})$ satisfies $[D _{p}] = \bar \tau _{p} ([D _{p}
^{\prime }])$, then exp$(D _{p}) = {\rm exp}(D _{p} ^{\prime }) =
{\rm exp}(D ^{\prime })$ and \par\noindent deg$(D _{p}) = {\rm
deg}(D _{p} ^{\prime }) = {\rm deg}(D ^{\prime })$. This completes
the proof of the inequality abrd$_{p}(E) \le {\rm abrd}_{p}(E
_{p})$, so Lemma \ref{lemm3.8} is proved.
\end{proof}
\par
\smallskip
\begin{lemm}
\label{lemm3.9} Let $E$ be a field, $p$ a prime number, $E _{p}$
the fixed field of some Sylow pro-$p$ subgroup of
$\mathcal{G}_{E}$, and $\mathbb{P} ^{\prime }$ the set of all $p'
\in \mathbb{P}$ different from $p$ and {\rm char}$(E)$. Denote by
$F$ and $F _{1}$ the rational function fields $E(X)$ and $E _{\rm
sep}(X)$, where $X$ is a transcendental element over $E$. Then
{\rm abrd}$_{p}(E) \le {\rm Brd}_{p}(F)$, {\rm abrd}$_{p}(E
_{p}(X)) = {\rm abrd}_{p}(F)$, and {\rm abrd}$_{p'}(E _{p}(X)) =
0$, for every prime $p' \in \mathbb{P} ^{\prime }$.
\end{lemm}
\par
\smallskip
\begin{proof}
The inequality abrd$_{p}(E) \le {\rm Brd}_{p}(F)$ is a special
case of Theorem~2.1 (a) and (b) of \cite{Ch4}. The proof of the
equality abrd$_{p}(E _{p}(X)) = {\rm abrd}_{p}(F)$ relies on the
fact that $E$ does not admit proper algebraic extensions in $F$,
which allows to deduce from Galois theory (cf. \cite[Ch. VI,
Theorem~1.12]{L}) that there exists a canonical group isomorphism
$\varphi \colon \mathcal{G}_{E} \to \mathcal{G}(F _{1}/F)$, such
that the image of $\mathcal{G}_{E'}$ under $\varphi $ coincides
with $\mathcal{G}(F _{1}/E ^{\prime }(X))$, for each $E ^{\prime }
\in I(E _{\rm sep}/E)$. At the same time, it becomes clear that
the mapping of $I(E _{\rm sep}/E)$ into $I(F _{1}/E)$, defined by
the rule $E ^{\prime } \to E ^{\prime }(X)$, is a
degree-preserving and (set-theoretic) inclusion-preserving
bijection. Therefore, it is easy to see that $\mathcal{G}(F _{1}/E
_{p}(X))$ is a Sylow pro-$p$ subgroup of $\mathcal{G}(F _{1}/F)$;
in particular, $p$ does not divide the degree of any finite
extension of $F$ in $E _{p}(X)$, which reduces the equality
abrd$_{p}(E _{p}(X)) = {\rm abrd}_{p}(F)$ to a consequence of
Lemma \ref{lemm3.8}. Now fix an algebraic closure $\overline E$ of
$E$, and an arbitrary finite extension $E _{p} ^{\prime }$ of $E
_{p}(X)$ in $F _{\rm sep}$, and put $\widetilde E _{p} = E _{p}
^{\prime }F _{1}$. We prove that abrd$_{p'}(E _{p}(X)) = 0$, for
all $p' \in \mathbb{P} ^{\prime }$, by showing that Br$(E _{p}
^{\prime }) _{p'} = \{0\}$. It is easily verified that $\widetilde
E _{p}/F _{1}$ is a finite extension, so it follows from Tsen's
theorem that Br$(\widetilde E _{p}) = \{0\}$ in case char$(E) =
0$. If char$(E) = q > 0$, then $\widetilde E _{p}F _{1,{\rm
ins}}/F _{1,{\rm ins}}$ is a finite extension, $\widetilde E _{p}F
_{1,{\rm ins}} = \widetilde E _{p,{\rm ins}}$, and the set $I(F
_{1,{\rm ins}}/F _{1})$ contains an isomorphic copy of the field
$\overline E(X)$; this allows to deduce from Tsen's theorem that
Br$(\widetilde E _{p,{\rm ins}}) = \{0\}$. Hence, by Lemma
\ref{lemm3.7} (a), the assertion that Br$(\widetilde E _{p}) _{p'}
= \{0\}$ holds in general, for all $p' \in (\mathbb{P} \setminus
\{{\rm char}(E)\})$. This means that $\widetilde E _{p}$ is a
splitting field of every $\Delta \in d(E _{p} ^{\prime })$ of
degree not divisible by char$(E)$, which requires the existence of
a finite extension $E _{p} ^{\prime }(\Delta )$ of $E _{p}
^{\prime }$ in $\widetilde E _{p}$ that is a splitting field of
$\Delta $ (see \cite[Sect. 13.2, Proposition~b]{P} or
\cite[(1.3)]{Ch2}). Taking finally into account that $\widetilde E
_{p}/E _{p} ^{\prime }$ is a Galois extension with
$\mathcal{G}(\widetilde E _{p}/E _{p} ^{\prime })$ a pro-$p$
group, one concludes that finite extensions of $E _{p} ^{\prime }$
in $\widetilde E _{p}$ are of $p$-power degrees. These
observations show that if char$(E) \nmid {\rm deg}(\Delta )$, then
deg$(\Delta )$ is a $p$-power, which implies Br$(E _{p} ^{\prime
}) _{p'} = \{0\}$, for all $p' \in \mathbb{P} ^{\prime }$, and so
completes the proof of Lemma \ref{lemm3.9}.
\end{proof}
\par
\medskip
\section{\bf Proof of Theorem \ref{theo1.1}}
\par
\medskip
For any field $K$ with a (nontrivial) Krull valuation $v$, $v(K)$
denotes the value group of $(K, v)$, $O _{v}(K) = \{a \in K\colon
\ v(a) \ge 0\}$ is the valuation ring of $(K, v)$, $M _{v}(K) =
\{\mu \in K\colon \ v(\mu ) > 0\}$ the maximal ideal of $O
_{v}(K)$, and $\widehat K = O _{v}(K)/M _{v}(K)$ the residue field
of $(K, v)$. As usual, $v(K)$ is assumed to be an ordered abelian
group; also, $\overline {v(K)}$ stands for a fixed divisible hull
of $v(K)$. The valuation $v$ is said to be Henselian if it extends
uniquely, up-to equivalence, to a valuation $v _{L}$ on each
algebraic extension $L$ of $K$. When this holds, $(K, v)$ is
called a Henselian field. It is known that $(K, v)$ is Henselian
if it is maximally complete, i.e. it has no valued extension $(K
^{\prime }, v')$ such that $K ^{\prime } \neq K$, $\widehat K
^{\prime } = \widehat K$ and $v'(K ^{\prime }) = v(K)$ (cf.
\cite[Theorem~15.3.5]{E3}). \label{max_comp} For example, complete
discrete valued fields are maximally complete, and for each $n \in
\mathbb{N}$, so is the $n$-fold iterated Laurent series field $K
_{n} = K _{0}((X _{1})) \dots ((X _{n}))$ over a field $K _{0}$
(that is, $K _{m} = K _{m-1}((X _{m}))$, for every index $m > 0$)
with respect to its standard valuation $v _{n}$ inducing on $K
_{0}$ the trivial valuation (see \cite[Exercise~3.11]{TW}, and
\cite[Theorem~18.4.1]{E3}). Here $v _{n}(K _{n}) = \mathbb{Z}
^{n}$, $K _{0}$ is the residue field of $(K _{n}, v _{n})$, and
the abelian group $\mathbb{Z} ^{n}$ is considered with its
inverse-lexicographic ordering (see \cite[Examples~4.2.2 and
9.2.2]{E3}).
\par
When $v$ is Henselian, so is $v _{L}$, for any algebraic field
extension $L/K$. In this case, we denote by $\widehat L$ the
residue field of $(L, v _{L})$, and put $v(L) = v _{L}(L)$,
\par\noindent
$O _{v}(L) = O _{v _{L}}(L)$. Clearly, $\widehat L/\widehat K$ is
an algebraic extension and $v(K)$ is an ordered subgroup of
$v(L)$, such that $v(L)/v(K)$ is a torsion group; hence, one may
assume without loss of generality that \label{k99} $v(L)$ is an
ordered subgroup of $\overline {v(K)}$. By Ostrowski's theorem
(cf. \cite[Theorem~17.2.1]{E3}), if $[L\colon K]$ is finite, then
it is divisible by $[\widehat L\colon \widehat K]e(L/K)$, where
$e(L/K)$ is the index of $v(K)$ in $v(L)$. The equality $[L\colon
K] = [\widehat L\colon \widehat K]e(L/K)$ holds if char$(\widehat
K) \nmid [L\colon K]$ (by Ostrowski's theorem), or else, if $v$ is
discrete and $L/K$ is separable (see \cite[Sect. 17.4]{E3}). We
say that $L/K$ is inertial if $[L\colon K] = [\widehat L\colon
\widehat K]$ and $\widehat L/\widehat K$ is separable (when this
holds, $L/K$ is separable). Inertial extensions have the following
properties (for a proof, see \cite[Theorem~A.23 and
Corollary~A.25]{TW}):
\par
\medskip
\begin{lemm}
\label{lemm4.3} Let $(K, v)$ be a Henselian field and $K _{\rm
ur}$ the compositum of inertial extensions of $K$ in $K _{\rm
sep}$. Then:
\par
{\rm (a)} An inertial extension $R ^{\prime }/K$ is Galois if and
only if so is $\widehat R ^{\prime }/\widehat K$. When this holds,
$\mathcal{G}(R ^{\prime }/K)$ and $\mathcal{G}(\widehat R ^{\prime
}/\widehat K)$ are canonically isomorphic.
\par
{\rm (b)} $v(K _{\rm ur}) = v(K)$, $K _{\rm ur}/K$ is a Galois
extension and $\mathcal{G}(K _{\rm ur}/K) \cong
\mathcal{G}_{\widehat K}$.
\par
{\rm (c)} Finite extensions of $K$ in $K _{\rm ur}$ are inertial, and
the natural mapping of $I(K _{\rm ur}/K)$ into $I(\widehat K _{\rm
sep}/\widehat K)$, by the rule $L \to \widehat L$, is bijective.
\par
{\rm (d)} For each finite extension $K _{1}$ of $K$ in $K _{\rm
sep}$, the field $K _{0} = K _{1} \cap K _{\rm ur}$ equals the
maximal inertial extension of $K$ in $K _{1}$; in addition,
$\widehat K _{0} = \widehat K _{1}$.
\end{lemm}
\par
\medskip
Note that if $(K, v)$ is a discrete valued field, then $O _{v}(K)$
is an excellent ring if and only if $K _{v}/K$ is a separable
(generally, transcendental) field extension, in the sense of
\cite[Ch.~VIII, Proposition~4.1]{L} and \cite[Lemma~2.6.1]{FJ}
(see also \cite[Ch.~4, Example~2.9, and Ch.~8, page~342 and
Corollary~2.40~(b)]{Li}). Our next lemma gives an example of such
ring, which allows us to deduce Theorem \ref{theo2.1} from Lemmas
\ref{lemm3.8}, \ref{lemm3.9} and the HHKL-theorem.
\par
\medskip
\begin{lemm}
\label{lemm4.4} Let $E$ be a field with char$(E) = q > 0$ and
$[E\colon E ^{q}] = q ^{\delta }$, for some integer $\delta \ge
0$, and let $U$ be an extension of $E$ in $E _{\rm sep}$, $E _{1}
= E((Z))$ and $U _{1} = U((Z))$ be Laurent series fields in an
indeterminate $Z$ over $E$ and $U$, respectively. Put $W = U.E
_{1}$, and denote by $e$ and $u$ the standard (Henselian)
$\mathbb{Z}$-valued valuations of $E _{1}$ and $U _{1}$, trivial
on $E$ and $U$, respectively. Then $w(W) = \mathbb{Z}$ and the
ring $O _{w}(W)$ is excellent, where $w = e _{W}$.
\end{lemm}
\par
\smallskip
\begin{proof} Put $B _{1} = B \cup \{Z\}$, where $B$ is a $q$-basis
of $E$ over $E ^{q}$ or $B = \emptyset $ depending on whether or
not $E \neq E ^{q}$. It is known that $U = U ^{q}$ if $E = E
^{q}$, and $B$ is a $q$-basis of $U$ over $U ^{q}$ in case $E \neq
E ^{q}$ (cf. \cite[Lemma~2.7.3]{FJ}); in particular, $[U\colon U
^{q}] = q ^{\delta }$. Note also that $E _{1} ^{q} = E ^{q}((Z
^{q}))$, $U _{1} ^{q} = U ^{q}((Z ^{q}))$, and $W ^{q} = U ^{q}.E
_{1} ^{q}$, which implies $B _{1}$ is a $q$-basis of $E _{1}$, $U
_{1}$ and $W$ over $E _{1} ^{q}$, $U _{1} ^{q}$ and $W ^{q}$,
respectively. At the same time, it is easily verified that
\par\noindent
$O _{e}(E _{1}) = E[[Z]]$, $O _{u}(U _{1}) = U[[Z]]$, and $O
_{w}(W) = U.E[[Z]] = U ^{q}.E[[Z]]$, where $w = e _{W}$. This
shows that $\widehat W = O _{w}(W)/M _{w}(W) = U$, $[\widehat
W\colon \widehat W ^{q}] = q ^{\delta }$, $[W\colon W ^{q}] = q
^{1+\delta }$, $u$ is the continuous prolongation of $w$ upon $U
_{1}$, the set $O _{w}(W) ^{q} = \{r ^{q}\colon r \in O _{w}(W)\}$
is a subring of $O _{w}(W)$ equal to $U ^{q}.E ^{q}[[Z ^{q}]]$,
and $O _{w}(W)$ is a free $O _{w}(W) ^{q}$-module of rank $q
^{\delta +1}$. Hence, by the Kunz theorem \cite[Theorem~2.5]{Kunz},
$O _{w}(W)$ is an excellent ring; equvalently, one obtains that $U
_{1}/W$ is a separable extension (which is algebraic if and only if
$[U\colon F] < \infty $).
\end{proof}
\par
\medskip
We are now prepared to complete the proof of Theorem
\ref{theo2.1}. Put
\par\noindent
$K = \mathcal{K}_{m} = K _{m}(X)$, where $X$ is an indeterminate
over $K _{m}$. We show that $b _{p}(K _{m}) \le b _{p}(K _{0}) +
m$, for each $p \in \mathbb{P}_{q}$, and in case $q
> 0$, abrd$_{q}(\mathcal{K}_{m}) < \infty $.
\par\smallskip
Suppose first that $m = 1$, char$(K _{1}) = 0$, and char$(K _{0}) = q
> 0$. By definition, $K _{1}$ is complete with respect to a discrete
valuation $v _{1}$ such that $\widehat K _{1} = K _{0}$. Note
further that $\mathcal{K}_{0} \in \Phi _{\rm Br}$, which ensures
that $K _{0}$ is a virtually perfect field (cf.
\cite[Theorem~2.1~(c)]{Ch4}), so it follows from
\cite[Theorem~3]{PS} that abrd$_{q}(K) < \infty $. Henceforth, we
assume that $p \neq q$ and $K _{p}$ is the fixed field of some
Sylow pro-$p$ subgroup $G _{p}$ of $\mathcal{G}_{K}$. Consider the
fields $\Theta _{p} = K _{1,{\rm sep}}(X) \cap K _{p}$,
$\mathcal{U}_{p} = K _{1,{\rm ur}}(X) \cap K _{p}$, $K _{1,p} =
\Theta _{p} \cap K _{1,{\rm sep}}$ and $U _{p} = \mathcal{U} _{p}
\cap K _{1,{\rm ur}}$. Using Galois theory and Sylow's theorems
for profinite groups as in the proof of Lemma \ref{lemm3.9}, one
obtains that $\Theta _{p} = K _{1,p}(X)$, $\mathcal{U}_{p} = U
_{p}(X)$, and $\mathcal{G}_{K _{1,p}}$ and $\mathcal{G}(K _{1,{\rm
ur}}/U _{p})$ are Sylow pro-$p$ subgroups of $\mathcal{G}_{K
_{1}}$ and $\mathcal{G}(K _{1,{\rm ur}}/K _{1})$, respectively.
Therefore, by Lemma \ref{lemm3.8}, abrd$_{p}(K) = {\rm
abrd}_{p}(\Theta _{p}) = {\rm abrd}_{p}(\mathcal{U}_{p})$, and by
Lemma \ref{lemm4.3}, the valuation of $U _{p}$ extending $v _{1}$
is Henselian and discrete, $\widehat U _{p} \in I(K _{0,{\rm
sep}}/K _{0})$, and $\mathcal{G}_{\widehat U _{p}}$ is a Sylow
pro-$p$ subgroup of $\mathcal{G}_{K_{0}}$. Let now $\widehat U
_{p}(X _{0})$ be a rational function field in an indeterminate $X
_{0}$ over $\widehat U _{p}$. Then, by Lemma \ref{lemm3.9},
abrd$_{p}(\widehat U _{p}(X _{0})) = {\rm abrd}_{p}(K _{0}(X
_{0}))$ and abrd$_{p'}(\widehat U _{p}(X _{0})) = 0$, for every
$p' \in \mathbb{P}$ with $p \neq p' \neq q$. Note also that
abrd$_{p}(\widehat U _{p}) = {\rm abrd}_{p}(K _{0})$, by Lemma
\ref{lemm3.8}, which ensures that $b _{p}(\widehat U _{p}) = b
_{p}(K _{0})$. Taking into account that the ring $O _{v
_{1}}(\widehat U _{p})$ is excellent (see
\cite[Corollary~8.2.40~(c)]{Li}), one obtains from the
HHKL-theorem that $b _{p}(U _{p}) \le b _{p}(K _{0}) + 1$. Thus
Theorem \ref{theo2.1} is proved in the special case of $m = 1$ and
char$(K _{1}) = 0 \neq q$.
\par
\smallskip
Next it is obtained by induction on $m$ that, for the rest of our
proof, one may assume that char$(K _{m}) = {\rm char}(K _{0}) = q$
and $K _{0}$ is a virtually perfect field. In view of the
Hasse-Schmidt theorem (see \cite[Proposition~A.5.3]{GiSz}, and
\cite[references on page~110]{E3}), this means to consider only
the case where $K _{m} = K _{0}((Y _{1})) \dots ((Y _{m}))$. Then
$K _{m-j} = K _{0}((Y _{1})) \dots ((Y _{m-j}))$ is the $j$-th
residue field of $K _{m}$, and it is virtually perfect, for each
index $j > 0$; hence, by Lemma \ref{lemm3.7}, abrd$_{q}(K _{m}) <
\infty $ when $q > 0$. As in the case of $m = 1$, fix an arbitrary
$p \in \mathbb{P}$, denote by $K _{0,p}$ the fixed field of some
Sylow pro-$p$ subgroup of $\mathcal{G}_{K _{0}}$, and identifying
$K _{0,{\rm sep}}$ with its $K _{0}$-isomorphic copy in $K
_{m,{\rm sep}}$, put $L _{m-j} = K _{0,p}K _{m-j}$, $0 \le j \le m
- 1$. Also, let $v _{j}$ be the prolongation on $L _{m-j}$ of the
standard $\mathbb{Z}$-valued valuation of $K _{m-j}$ that is
trivial on $K _{m-j-1}$, for each index $j < m$. Clearly, $L
_{m-j}$ is an algebraic extension of $K _{m-j}$ generated by $K
_{0,p}$, $(L _{m-j}, v _{j})$ is a Henselian discrete valued field
with $\widehat L _{m-j} = L _{m-j-1}$, and by Lemma \ref{lemm4.4}
and \cite[Corollary~8.2.40]{Li}, $O _{v _{j}}(L _{m-j})$ is an
excellent ring. Since $K _{0}$ has no proper algebraic extensions
in $K _{m}$, this implies $p$ does not divide the degree of any
finite extension of $K _{m}$ in $L _{m}$. Therefore, it follows
from Lemmas \ref{lemm3.8} and \ref{lemm3.9} that abrd$_{p}(L
_{0}(X)) = {\rm abrd}_{p}(K _{0}(X)) < \infty $, and abrd$_{p'}(L
_{0}(X)) = 0$, for every prime $p' \neq p$. Similarly, it can be
deduced from Lemma \ref{lemm3.9} that abrd$_{p}(L _{m}) = {\rm
abrd}_{p}(K _{m})$. Thus the inequalities abrd$_{p}(L _{m}(X)) <
\infty $ and $b _{p}(K _{m}) \le b _{p}(K _{0}) + m$ reduce to
consequences of the HHKL-theorem. As $p$ is an arbitrary element
of $\mathbb{P}_{q}$ and $K _{m}$ is virtually perfect, this result
and Lemma \ref{lemm3.7} show that $K = K _{m}(X)$ is a $\Phi _{\rm
Br}$-field, so Theorem \ref{theo2.1} is proved.
\par
\medskip
\begin{rema}
\label{rema4.4} The assertion of Theorem \ref{theo2.1} in case
char$(K _{m}) = {\rm char}(K _{0})$ is an immediate consequence of
the HHKL-theorem (when $q > 0$, combined with Lemma \ref{lemm3.7}
and the equality $[K\colon K ^{q}] = q ^{m+1}$) if $K _{0}$ is a
finite or algebraically closed field; if $m = 1$, char$(K _{1}) =
0$ and char$(K _{0}) = q
> 0$, then it follows directly from \cite[Theorem~3]{PS}. The same
holds if $K _{0}$ is a perfect PAC field, since then abrd$_{p}(K
_{0}) \le 1$, for all $p \in \mathbb{P}$ (see
\cite[Lemma~4.8]{Ch7}).
\end{rema}
\par
\medskip
Note finally that the generalized Artin conjecture (the original one,
proposed by M. Artin, concerns the case of $d = 2$, see
\cite[Sect.~4]{ABGV}) states that if $K$ is a $C _{d}$-field, for
some $d > 0$, then abrd$_{p}(K) \le d - 1$, for all $p \in
\mathbb{P}$. When $K _{0}$ is a finitely-generated extension of a
finite or algebraically closed field $E$, such that trd$(K _{0}/E)
= n > 0$, it predicts that abrd$_{p}(K _{0}) = e\colon p \in
\mathbb{P}$, where $e = n$ in the former case, and $e = n - 1$ in
the latter one. In view of \cite[Theorem~3]{PS}, and the
HHKL-theorem, the validity of this conjecture, for a given $n$,
would allow to prove (adapting the examples in the Appendix to
\cite{Li11} to finite extensions of $\mathcal{K}_{0}$ and
$\mathcal{K}_{m}$, see \cite[Proposition~5.9]{Ch4} and
\cite[(3.1)]{Ch3}) that Brd$_{p}(K _{0} ^{\prime }) = e + 1$ and
Brd$_{p}(K) = e + m + 1$, $p \in \mathbb{P}$, whenever $K _{0}
^{\prime }/K _{0}$ and $K/K _{m}$ are finitely-generated
extensions, trd$(K _{0} ^{\prime }/K _{0}) = {\rm trd}(K/K _{m}) =
1$, and $K _{m}$ is an $m$-local field with an $m$-th residue
field $K _{0}$, for some $m \in \mathbb{N}$. The conjecture holds
in case $n = 1$, by Tsen's theorem and class field theory (cf.
\cite[Sect. 19.4]{P} and \cite[(32.19)]{Re}); in case $n = 2$, it
has been proved in \cite{Jong} and \cite{Li08} if $E$ is
algebraically closed, and in \cite{Li15} when $E$ is finite.
\par
