\section{Proposed Method}
These conference call transcripts present significant analytical challenges, typically containing over 5,000 words with substantial redundancy and industry-specific terminology that creates obstacles for language models. These lengthy documents contain both valuable predictive signals as well as considerable noise. When machine learning models inadvertently capture this noise rather than meaningful patterns, they form spurious correlations with class labels, severely compromising their generalization ability and real-world utility.
This fundamental challenge motivates our approach of utilizing SAEs for feature selection. 

We propose \emph{Sparse Autoencoder for Financial Representation Enhancement (SAE-FiRE)}, which leverages the inherent capacity of SAEs to identify and isolate essential patterns while filtering out noise.  Our SAE-FiRE  systematically removes redundant noise that might otherwise lead to overfitting, ultimately producing more robust and generalizable earnings surprise predictions. SAE-FiRE contains the following three steps (see Figure~\ref{fig:method-arch}). 

\begin{figure*}[ht]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{architecture7.pdf}
  \caption{Diagram of our SAE‐based earnings‐surprise prediction pipeline.}
  \label{fig:method-arch}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Obtaining Representations} 
We utilize the pretrained SAEs released by Gemma Scope~\cite{lieberum2024gemma}. For every earnings conference‐call transcript (truncated to \(T=20{,}000\) tokens), we extract each token’s residual‐stream activations from the layers instrumented with the SAELens tool~\cite{bloom2024saetrainingcodebase}.  Denote by:
$
\mathbf{h}_i \in \mathbb{R}^d
$,
the \(d\)-dimensional SAE feature vector at token position \(i\), for \(i=1,2,\dots,T\).

To collapse these token‐wise representations into a single, fixed‐length embedding for the entire document, we apply a simple sum‐pooling operator:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{s}
\;=\;
\sum_{i=1}^{T} \mathbf{h}_i
\quad\in\;\mathbb{R}^d.
\label{eq:sum_pooling}
\end{equation}
Here, \(\mathbf{s}\) serves as the global SAE signature of the transcript, aggregating activation strength across all token positions without any learned parameters. This summation‐based pooling not only enforces a consistent input size for downstream classifiers but also emphasizes the cumulative contribution of each latent feature over the full document.


\subsection{Feature Selection via SAE}  
After sum‐pooling, each transcript is represented by a \(d\)–dimensional vector \(\mathbf{s}\).  Each earnings conference call transcript is joined to its SUE score based on the EPS reporting of the immediately following quarter from the earnings surprise database. We labeled the samples with \(\text{SUE}\ge0.5\) as `positive', denoting positive earnings surprise, and \(\text{SUE}\le-0.5\) as `negative', denoting negative earnings surprise, discarding those in \((-0.5,0.5)\) as non-surprise.  To quantify the ability of each SAE feature to separate two classes, we compute three univariate scores:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]\setlength\itemsep{-0.3em}
  \item \(F\)–test (ANOVA).  
    Viewing the two groups as levels of a factor, the \(F\)–statistic compares between‐group variance to within‐group variance:
    \[
      F_j \;=\; \frac{\bigl(\bar{s}_{j,+}-\bar{s}_j\bigr)^2 + \bigl(\bar{s}_{j,-}-\bar{s}_j\bigr)^2}{\tfrac1{n_+ + n_- -2}\sum_{i}(s_{j}^{(i)}-\bar{s}_{\text{group}})^2},
    \]
    where \(\bar{s}_j\) is the overall mean.  Higher \(F_j\) signals greater dispersion between classes, though it can be sensitive to outliers.

  % \item \textbf{Mutual Information (MI).}  
  %   Treating feature \(s_j\) and label \(Y\) as random variables, we estimate
  %   \[
  %     I(s_j;Y)
  %     = \sum_{y\in\{+,-\}}\!\int p(s_j,y)\,\log\frac{p(s_j,y)}{p(s_j)p(y)}\,\mathrm{d}s_j.
  %   \]
  %   MI captures any dependency (linear or non‐linear) between \(s_j\) and \(Y\), but requires careful density estimation and more samples to avoid bias.

    \item Tree‐Based Methods.
    We train an ensemble of decision trees using gradient boosting to predict the binary surprise label \(Y\) from the full activation vector \(\mathbf{s}\). For each feature \(j\), we record its average importance score, such as mean decrease in impurity or permutation importance, across all trees. Features with higher importance contribute more to reducing prediction error and may capture complex, non‐linear interactions.
\end{itemize}

Each method produces a score per feature. We then rank the \(d\) features accordingly and retain the top-\(k\) for downstream classification to reduce noise and facilitate efficient classifier training. By filtering out redundant and spurious activations, our approach yields more robust and generalizable feature sets. We experimented with feature selection using each of these two methods to assess their relative effectiveness.


\subsection{Fitting A Classifier}  
Using the top-\(k\) SAE features ranked by our selection criteria, we train logistic regression classifiers to predict positive versus negative earnings surprises.  For each transcript, the pooled SAE vector \(\mathbf{s}\) is reduced to its \(k\) most discriminative dimensions, and these \(k\) features are used as input to a logistic regression model with an \(\ell_2\)-regularized loss.  We evaluate four choices of \(k\in\{500,\,1000,\,1500,\,2000\}\) to assess the impact of feature set size on predictive performance.  All classifiers are trained and tested under 5-fold cross-validation, and hyperparameters (including the regularization strength) are selected via grid search on each training fold.  Model accuracy, precision, recall, weighted average F1-score, and area under the ROC curve are reported to compare the effectiveness of different \(k\) values in capturing salient SAE activations for earnings-surprise classification.  



