\subsection{Comparison with Baseline Models}
In this section, we compare SAE-FiRE with baselines and the results are given in Figure~\ref{fig:all_score}.

\vspace{3pt}
\noindent\textbf{Comparison to Hidden State Probes.}
When Gemma 2-2B is used, SAE-FiRE model (top \(k=1500\) features, tree-based feature selection) achieves a weighted‐average F1 of 0.743, ROC AUC of 0.657, and accuracy of 0.793. In contrast, logistic‐regression and MLP probes on the last‐hidden‐state embeddings of Gemma 2-2B yield lower scores (weighted F1: 0.737 and 0.731; AUC: 0.628 and 0.634; accuracy: 0.761 and 0.770, respectively). These results demonstrate that sum‐pooled SAE activations capture cumulative, global patterns across the transcript, whereas final‐token embeddings miss broader contextual cues. Additionally, univariate feature selection prunes spurious or redundant dimensions, sharpening class separation and improving generalization.

\vspace{3pt}

\noindent\textbf{Benefit of Selecting the Most Important SAE Activation Features.}
Training on the full 16K‐dimensional SAE vectors from Gemma 2-2B layer 12 without feature selection yields a weighted F1 of 0.737, AUC of 0.642, and an accuracy of 0.774 (MLP), and weighted F1 of 0.716, AUC of 0.621, and an accuracy of 0.782 (XGBoost). By contrast, our SAE-FiRE models (Gemma 2‐2B, \(k=1500\), tree-based feature selection) achieves a weighted‐average F1 of 0.743, ROC AUC of 0.657, and accuracy of 0.793. This indicates filtering to the most discriminative features reduces overfitting to document‐specific noise. Besides, classifiers on 1.5K dimensions train faster and require less memory than on 16K.

\vspace{3pt}
\subsection{Influence of Feature Selection}
In this section, we investigate the influence of feature importance ranking methods. We report the results in Figure~\ref{fig:feature-selection}.

\vspace{3pt}
\noindent\textbf{Comparison of Feature Selection Methods.}
We compare the model performance using two different feature selection methods: ANOVA F-tests, and tree-based method. The result shows that models trained with features selected using the tree-based approach outperforms models using features selected using ANOVA for both the model based on 16K SAE from Gemma 2-2B and the model based on 131K SAE from Gemma 2-9B. For example, for SAE-FiRE with Gemma 2-9B 131K SAE, the weighted F1 is 0.757, the AUC is 0.668, and the accuracy is 0.801 when using tree-based method, compared to the weighted F1 of 0.748, AUC of 0.664, and accuracy of 0.790 when using ANOVA. For SAE-FiRE with Gemma 2-2B 16K SAE, the weighted F1 is 0.743, the AUC is 0.657, and the accuracy is 0.793 when using tree-based method, compared to the weighted F1 of 0.743, AUC of 0.646, and accuracy of 0.788 when using ANOVA. 

\vspace{3pt}
\noindent\textbf{Comparison of Number of Features.}
For SAE-FiRE with Gemma 2-2B 16K, we test the number of top features $k\in\{500, 1000, 1500, 2000, 2500\}$, and we observe a peak of performance at around $k = 1500$. For Gemma 2-9B 131K , we test $k\in\{3000, 3500, 4000, 4500, 5000, 5500, 6000\}$, and we observe a peak of performance at around $k = 4500$. These findings suggest that \emph{there is an optimal feature subset size}. When the number of features is smaller, the model cannot access all of the discriminative signals by omitting useful predictors, whereas when too many less important features are introduced, noises increase, which has a negative effect on classification.

