%First, both instrument strength and the disturbance in the first-stage equation reinforce the power of the test. Specifically, the instrument strength affects the power through $\pi'S_N^2\pi$ and $\pi'S_N\pi$, while the first-stage disturbance affects the power through $v'\underline{S}_Nv$. In particular, we expect the power of the test to remain even when the instruments are relatively weak, due to the contribution of the disturbance part. This is very different from the standard power theory under many weak instruments with independent data (e.g., see \cite{MS22}, \cite{Lim2024}, and \cite{LWZ2024dimension}). Second, by Cauchy-Schwartz's inequality and Markov's inequality, $\left(v'\varepsilon\right)^{2}\leq N^{2}\frac{1}{N}v^{'}v\cdot\frac{1}{N}\varepsilon^{'}\varepsilon=O_{\mathbb{P}}\left(N^{2}\right)$. It is observed from the second component of $\varpi_{N}$ that the correlation between the two error processes $\varepsilon$ and $v$ also plays a crucial role in power determination. A negative correlation (i.e., a negative degrees of endogeneity) improves power. 
