Next, we consider the Freundlich isotherm case, where \(\phi'(c)\) tends to infinity as \(c \to 0\), resulting in a degenerate adsorption problem.
\begin{example}
Consider a Dirichlet problem on \([-3,3]\), with the analytical solution \(c(x,t) = exp(-t)\tanh^2(2x)\). Set \(u = x\), \(D = x^2 + 1\), and \(\phi(c) = c^p\) with \(p = \frac{1}{3}\). The source term \(f(x,t)\) and boundary conditions follow directly from the analytical solution. Results are evaluated at the terminal time \(T = 1\).
\end{example}
\par
Since \( {\phi}' (0)=\infty \), the problem is degenerate. Thus, we approximate \( \phi(c) \) by a regularized function \( \phi_\varepsilon(c) \) with bounded slope \cite{barrett1997finite,radu2010newton}, defined as
\begin{equation}
\phi_{\varepsilon}(c) = 
\begin{cases}
\phi(c), & c > \varepsilon, \\
p \varepsilon^{p-1}c + (1-p)\varepsilon^p, & c \in [0, \varepsilon],
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
The regularization parameter is set to \(\varepsilon = 10^{-10}\).
