In principle, $\mathcal F[\bar{\bm{W}}]$ is a random variable, but fortunately in the $N \to \infty$ limit, the free energy concentrates around its average $\mathcal F = \mathbb{E}_{\bar{\bm W}\sim p(\bar{\bm \theta}|\mathcal{T}_1)} \mathcal F[\bar{\bm{W}}]$ which can be computed using the replica trick \cite{charbonneau2023spin, franz2013quasi}. This average will capture the typical behavior of draws of $\bar{\bm W}$ from the posterior distribution of weights for the source task $\mathcal T_1$ under a Gaussian prior $\bar{W}_{ij}, \bar{w}_j\sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)$, which itself can be characterized using statistical mechanics \cite{lauditi2025adaptive} (see Appendix~\ref{appendix::bayes}). 
