\section{Transfer Learning Phenomenology}
\vspace{-5pt}
In the following, we illustrate the interplay between transfer learning, feature learning strength, sample size and task similarity leveraging our theoretical results in Section \ref{sec:theory}. We start with controlled settings where the data is generated by teacher-student setups of increasing complexity, and then conclude with real datasets.

\vspace{-5pt}
\subsection{Transfer Learning of Linear Tasks with Linear Activations}
\vspace{-5pt}
Linear neural networks (where $\phi(h) \equiv h$) are the simplest analytically tractable models of feature learning~\cite{saxe2014exactsolutionsnonlineardynamics, advani2017highdimensionaldynamicsgeneralizationerror, arora2019convergenceanalysisgradientdescent, aitchison2020biggerbetterfiniteinfinite,Li_2021,jacot2022saddletosaddledynamicsdeeplinear,Zavatone_Veth_2022}. In our setting, given linear activations, the distribution of preactivations remains Gaussian at each layer when $P=\Theta_N(1)$ due to the Central Limit Theorem. Because of this property, in the Bayesian framework the saddle point equations for the kernels of the downstream task ($\mathcal{T}_2$) simplify (see Appendix~\ref{appendix::bayesian_linearNN}):
\begin{align}\label{eq::tl_linear_sp}
        \bm \Phi &= \left[ (1+\delta) \bm C^{-1} + \hat{\bm\Phi} \right]^{-1} + \delta^2 \left[ (1+\delta) \bm C^{-1} + \hat{\bm\Phi} \right]^{-1} \bm{C}^{-1} \mathbb{E}_{\bar{\bm h}\sim p(\bar{\bm h})} (\bar{\h}_{2} \bar{\h}_{2}^\top)  \bm{C}^{-1}  \left[ (1+\delta) \bm C^{-1} + \hat{\bm\Phi} \right]^{-1}  \nonumber \\
        \hat{\bm \Phi} &= -\gamma_0^2 \Big(\frac{\bm{I}}{\beta}+\bm{\Phi}\Big)^{-1}\bm{y}\bm{y}^{\top}\Big(\frac{\bm{I}}{\beta}+\bm{\Phi}\Big)^{-1},
\end{align}
%
where in the above equation $\bm C = \bm C^{(2,2)}$ is the target task Gram matrix (Eq.~\eqref{eq::datagram}) and $\bm y$ is the target-task labels. The dependence on the source task enters through $\bar{\h}_2 \in \mathbb{R}^{P_2}$, which are the preactivations for the source model on target task $\mathcal T_2$ data. The expectation is taken over $p(\bar{\bm h})\sim \mathcal{N}\left(0,(\bm C_{\mathcal{T}_1 \cup \mathcal{T}_2}^{-1} + \bar{\bm \Phi}_ {\mathcal{T}_1} )^{-1}\right)$. 

It is instructive to consider the lazy limit where $\gamma_0 \to 0$. Here,  the dual kernel $\hat{\bm \Phi}$ vanishes and Eq.~\eqref{eq::tl_linear_sp} reduces to $\bm \Phi = \frac{1}{\left(1+\delta\right)}\bm C+\frac{\delta^2}{\left(1+\delta\right)^2}\mathbb{E}_{\bar{\bm h}}(\bar{\bm h}_{\mathcal T_2} \bar{\bm h}_{\mathcal T_2}^\top)  $. When $\delta\to\infty$,  the network is solving a kernel regression on $\mathcal{T}_2$ with the fixed (adaptive) feature kernel produced by the pre-training on $\mathcal{T}_1$. When $\delta\to 0$,  the network is solving a kernel regression on $\mathcal{T}_2$ with the fixed feature kernel induced by random linear features. For intermediate values of $\delta$, the network still solves a kernel regression problem with a kernel interpolating between these two limiting kernels. By contrast, in the feature‐learning regime ($\gamma_0 = \Theta_N (1)$) the downstream task feature adaptation and the elastic coupling $\delta$ interact in a non-trivial way, producing kernels that integrate representations on both $\mathcal{T}_1$ and $\mathcal{T}_2$.

A clear example of this is the simplest possible case where $\mathcal{T}_1 \equiv \mathcal{T}_2$, the input data are whitened $\bm C^{(1,1)} = \bm C^{(2,2)} = \bm I \in \mathbb{R}^{P \times P}$ and the labels are normalized $|\bm y |^2 = 1$. We show in Appendix~\ref{appendix::bayesian_linearNN}, that in this setting the equations get simplified even further since both source $\{ \bar{\bm \Phi}, \hat{\bar{\bm \Phi}}\}$ and target $\{\bm \Phi, \hat{\bm \Phi} \}$ kernels only grow in the rank-one direction $\bm y \bm y^{\top}$. As a consequence, one can only keep track of the overlaps with the label direction $\bm y^{\top} \bm \Phi \bm y = \phi$ and $\bm y^{\top} \hat{\bm \Phi} \bm y = \hat{\phi}$. For the target $\mathcal{T}_2$ we get
\begin{equation}
\phi = (1+\delta+\hat{\phi})^{-1} + \delta^2 \,\bar{\phi} \,(1+\delta+\hat{\phi})^{-2}, \qquad \hat{\phi} = -\gamma_0^2  (\beta^{-1} + \phi)^{-2}.
\end{equation}
The saddle point equations for the kernels of $\mathcal{T}_1$ were derived in~\cite{lauditi2025adaptive} and can be found in Appendix~\ref{appendix::bayes}. When $\delta \to \infty$ the target model strongly relies on the features learned from $\mathcal{T}_1$ and $\phi = \bar{\phi}$ as expected. We simulate this model in Figure~\ref{fig::same_dataset_main}, where we allow the pre-trained model to learn with a feature strength $\gamma_s$ different from $\gamma_t$ of the target model. When the source is richer than the target ($\gamma_s > \gamma_t$), increasing the elastic coupling monotonically increase the label alignment $\phi$. If instead the target is richer ($\gamma_s <\gamma_t$), the contrary happens and the optimum occurs at $\delta = 0$. In the balanced case $\gamma_s = \gamma_t$, $\phi$ remains almost flat, rising only marginally with $\delta$ under our low-data regime.
%When $\gamma_s > \gamma_t$, $\phi$ is an increasing function of $\delta$, since transfer learning has the effect of enhancing the overlap with the label direction because of the richness of the pre-trained model (this reflects in the block structure of the kernels in Fig.~\ref{fig::same_dataset_main}). When $\gamma_s < \gamma_t$ the opposite happens, and since the target model is richer than the source, the highest alignment with the labels is at $\delta = 0$. The case where $\gamma_s = \gamma_t$ tells the overlap $\phi$ only slightly increase with $\delta$ because the of the low data regime we are considering here. 

