\paragraph{Task similarity.} By slightly increasing the task complexity for the same linear models, we generate a dataset $\{\bm x_{\mu},y_{\mu}\}_{\mu=1}^{P_2}$ from a population distribution $\bm x_{\mu} \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\bm I_D)$ by setting $\bm y_{\mu} = \bm w \cdot \bm x_{\mu}$ with $||\bm w||_2 = 1$. Here, the task vector $\bm w$ is partially aligned with the source task vector $\bm \beta$ (s.t. $||\bm \beta||_2 = 1$) who generated the source labels of $\mathcal{T}_1$ according to $\bar{\bm y}_{\mu} = \bm \beta \cdot \bm x_{\mu}$, such that $\bm \beta \cdot \bm w = \alpha$. In Fig.~\ref{fig::same_dataset_diff_teachers} we show that, for $P_1 > P_2$ and $\gamma_s = \gamma_t$, the effect of transfer learning is to improve the test loss performance for $\alpha >0 $. When $\alpha = 0$ and the source and target task vectors are orthogonal, transfer learning has no effect on the test loss, because in order to interpolate on $\mathcal{T}_2$, the model needs to learn a new direction $\bm w$ without any prior knowledge. In the extreme case where $P_1 = P_2$ and $\bar{\bm y} \cdot \bm y = m$ the kernels can be studied by tracking their overlaps with the $\text{span}\{\bar{\bm y}, \bm y\}$ directions (see Appendix~\ref{appendix::bayesian_linearNN}). 

