\subsection{Transfer Learning of Polynomial Tasks with Nonlinear Activations}

\vspace{-5pt}
\paragraph{Low to High Degree Polynomials} 

%As mentioned in section~\ref{sec::dmft}, DMFT theory of transfer learning allows to track the evolution of preactivation and pregradient fields when learning the downstream task proceeds under gradient flow with weight decay and the elastic penalty $\delta$ (see Appendix~\ref{appendix::dmft}). 
%Here, because separation of time scales between the pre-trained and downstream tasks appears naturally given the time dependency of Eqs. ?, we can store $\mathcal{S}$ samples of the fields $\bar{ h}_{\mu \in \mathcal{T}_2}(t_1) \sim p(\bar{ h}_{\mu \in \mathcal{T}_2}(t_1))$ over $\mathcal{T}_2$ after solving the non-Markovian dynamics of Eq. ? for $\mathcal{T}_1$, and then use them as a warm start for the target task fields $\{h_{\mu \in \mathcal{T}_2}(0), g_{\mu \in \mathcal{T}_2}(0) \}$ who evolve according to the dynamics of Eq. ?. 

Kernel limits of neural networks are strongly biased to fit their data with low degree polynomials when data is high dimensional and isotropic. This spectral bias~\cite{rahaman2019spectralbiasneuralnetworks,bordelon2020spectrum,canatar2021spectral} reflects the fact that kernel methods learn eigenfunctions in order of decreasing eigenvalue~\cite{novak2018sensitivitygeneralizationneuralnetworks,Belkin_2019,Zhi_Qin_John_Xu_2020}. By contrast, networks trained in the feature-learning regime can learn sparse polynomials from much fewer data and training steps \cite{Mei2018, dandi2023twolayerneuralnetworkslearn,troiani2024fundamental, dandi2024random}. The staircase property~\cite{abbe2021staircasepropertyhierarchicalstructure,abbe2023sgdlearningneuralnetworks,abbe2024mergedstaircasepropertynecessarynearly,yang2025effectiverankstaircasephenomenon} explored by~\cite{dandi2023twolayerneuralnetworkslearn} makes this hierarchy explicit in multi-index polynomial settings. %In~\cite{dandi2023twolayerneuralnetworkslearn}, the authors show that an infinitely wide NN trained by gradient descent recovers each monomial term sequentially - at every step acquiring a new feature direction orthogonal to those already learned - thereby achieving full recovery with only $P = \Theta (D)$ samples. On the other hand, any target direction whose support is not linked by one of this staircase chain requires $P= \Theta (D^2)$ samples, giving a notion of complexity in polynomial tasks. 
%For this reason, here, we study the interplay between a data-scarce regime, feature learning in $\mu P$ parameterization, and the effect of transfer learning in improving sample efficiency and performance at test time. 

Inspired by the utility of feature learning on sparse polynomials of Gaussian data $\bm x \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\bm I)$, we explore transfer learning in this setting where learned features from the source task could promote performance on the target task. In Fig.~\ref{fig::polynomial_teacher} we show that first learning a linear function $y_1(\bm x) = \frac{1}{\sqrt D} \bm\beta \cdot \bm x$ before learning a quadratic teacher function $y_2(\bm x) = \left( \frac{1}{\sqrt D} \bm\beta \cdot \bm x \right)^2$ can improve performance when $\delta > 0$ and $P_2$ is small (Fig.~\ref{fig::polynomial_teacher}). The same happens with Hermite polynomial teachers $y_2(\bm x) = \text{He}_k \left( \frac{1}{\sqrt D} \bm \beta \cdot \bm x\right)$ of increasing degree $k$. Fig.~\ref{fig::lowtohigh_hermite} shows that learning and $\delta >0$ when the pre-trained task is of lower complexity than the target, allows the model to generalize better compared to the performance of a model randomly initialized ($\delta = 0$).

