\subsection{Role of Transfer Learning on real datasets}
\vspace{-5pt}
Moving beyond synthetic tasks, we consider simple image classification problems. We start with CIFAR-10, where a model pretrained on two source classes is fine-tuned on two disjoint target classes. In Fig.~\ref{fig::dmft}, for every feature‐learning strength $\gamma_{0}$ the downstream test loss falls monotonically as $\delta$ increases, indicating improvements due to transfer learning. The best performance is achieved at large $\delta$ and large $\gamma_{0}$, showing that the combination of strong elastic penalties and rich training yields the lowest loss. In Fig.~\ref{fig::dmft} the distribution of the preactivations $p(h)$ on the target shows that, as $\delta$ grows, $p(h)$ broadens and develops heavier tails. Intuitively, mixing in the pretrained filters injects higher‐variance components into the representation, so that activations span a wider range even as the network learns task‐specific structure.

