\subsubsection{Same Data, different labels}
Suppose again that 
\begin{align}
    \bm C_{\mathcal T_1 \cup \mathcal T_2} = \begin{bmatrix}
        \bm I & \bm I
        \\
        \bm I & \bm I
    \end{bmatrix} 
\end{align}
but that in principle, in this case,  

From the saddle point equations for $\mathcal{T}_1$, we know that
\begin{equation}
    \bar{\bm \Phi} = \bm I + (\bar{\phi}-1) \,\bm y_1 \bm y^{\top}_1
\end{equation}
and since the saddle point equations for $\mathcal{T}_2$ are
\begin{align}
    &\bm \Phi = \left[ (1+\eta) \bm I + \hat{\bm \Phi}\right]^{-1} + \eta^2 \left[ (1+\eta) \bm I + \hat{\bm \Phi}\right]^{-1} \left( \bm I + (\bar{\phi}-1) \,\bm y_1 \bm y^{\top}_1\right) \left[ (1+\eta) \bm I + \hat{\bm \Phi}\right]^{-1} \nonumber
    \\
    &\hat{\bm \Phi} = -\gamma_0^2 (\bm \Phi)^{-1} \bm y_2 \bm y_2^{\top} (\bm \Phi)^{-1}
\end{align}
one realizes that the only non-trivial contributions to $\bm \Phi$ comes from the $\text{span}\{ \bm y_1, \bm y_2\}$, so in principle one can decompose
\begin{align}
    \bm \Phi = a \,\bm I + b \,\bm y_1 \bm y_1^{\top} + c\, (\bm y_1 \bm y_2^{\top}+ \bm y_2 \bm y_1^{\top} )+ d \, \bm y_2 \bm y_2^{\top}
\end{align}
which means
\begin{equation}
    \bm \Phi = a \bm I + \begin{bmatrix}
        \bm y_1 & \bm y_2
    \end{bmatrix} 
    \begin{bmatrix}
        b & c
        \\
        c & d
    \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix}
        \bm y^{\top}_1 \\
        \bm y^{\top}_2
    \end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}
from which
\begin{align}
    \bm \Phi^{-1} = \left( a \bm I + \bm u \bm C \bm u^{\top}\right)^{-1} = a^{-1} \bm I - a^{-2} \bm u \left(\bm C^{-1} +a^{-1} \bm u^{\top} \bm u \right)^{-1}\bm u^{\top}
\end{align}
and 
\begin{align}
    \bm \Phi^{-1} \bm y_2 = a^{-1} \bm y_2 - a^{-2} \bm u \left(\bm C^{-1} +a^{-1} \bm u^{\top} \bm u \right)^{-1}\begin{bmatrix}
        \bm y^{\top}_1 \bm y_2\\
        1
    \end{bmatrix} 
\end{align}
being $\bm y^{\top}_1 \bm y_2 = m$. It turns out, one can solve for $\{a,b,c,d\}$ self consistently and for different values of $m$.


