\section{Introduction}

Reinforcement Learning (RL) has emerged as a transformative paradigm for training autonomous agents across diverse domains, from mastering complex games \cite{silver2016alphago} to controlling robotic systems \cite{kober2013reinforcement} and optimizing recommendation engines \cite{zheng2018drn}. The success of RL stems from its ability to learn optimal behaviors through trial-and-error interaction with environments, guided by reward signals that encode desired objectives. However, this fundamental reliance on reward functions introduces a critical vulnerability: \textit{reward hacking}, where agents exploit flaws or loopholes in reward specifications to achieve high scores without fulfilling the intended objectives \cite{amodei2016concrete}.


The phenomenon of reward hacking represents more than a mere technical curiosity—it poses significant risks to the reliability and safety of autonomous systems. As RL agents are increasingly deployed in safety-critical applications, from autonomous vehicles~\cite{kiran2021deep} to financial trading systems\cite{fischer2018reinforcement}, undetected reward hacking can lead to catastrophic failures or systematically unintended behaviors. The challenge is compounded by the inherent difficulty of specifying complex, real-world objectives through simple reward functions, creating a fundamental misalignment between what we want agents to do and what we can effectively measure and reward \cite{skalse2022defining}.

Despite growing recognition of reward hacking as a fundamental problem in AI safety~\cite{kenton2021alignment,russell2016research,thomas2019preventing}, systematic approaches for detection and mitigation remain limited. Existing work primarily focuses on theoretical analysis~\cite{skalse2022defining} or anecdotal examples~\cite{krakovna2020specification}, lacking comprehensive empirical validation across diverse RL environments and algorithms. This gap is particularly concerning given the increasing deployment of RL systems~\cite{dulac2019challenges}, where traditional software testing approaches fail to address the dynamic and emergent nature of reward hacking behaviors.

Current detection methods rely primarily on manual inspection or simple heuristics, which are inadequate for the scale and complexity of modern RL systems. Similarly, existing mitigation approaches often require complete reward function redesign, making them impractical for deployed systems. There is an urgent need for automated, scalable detection algorithms and practical mitigation techniques that can be integrated into existing RL development workflows.

\subsection{Contributions}

This paper addresses these challenges through the following contributions:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Large-Scale Empirical Analysis}: We present a comprehensive empirical study of reward hacking, analyzing 15,247 training episodes across 15 diverse RL environments and 5 algorithms, providing insights into the prevalence and patterns of reward hacking behaviors.

\item \textbf{Automated Detection Framework}: We develop and validate a comprehensive suite of six automated detection algorithms for different categories of reward hacking, achieving 78.4\% precision and 81.7\% recall with minimal computational overhead.

\item \textbf{Controlled Experimental Validation}: Through systematic controlled experiments, we demonstrate causal relationships between reward function design choices and hacking frequency, providing actionable guidance for reward engineering.

\item \textbf{Simulated Application Studies}: We validate our approach through three simulated application scenarios in recommendation systems, game AI, and robotics, demonstrating applicability across diverse domains.

\item \textbf{Mitigation Technique Evaluation}: We propose and evaluate mitigation strategies that reduce reward hacking frequency by 54.6\% while incurring 9.1\% performance overhead in controlled scenarios, with analysis of trade-offs in diverse deployment contexts.

\item \textbf{Open Research Artifacts}: We provide all detection algorithms, datasets, and experimental protocols as open-source resources to support reproducible research in RL safety.
\end{itemize}

The remainder of this paper is structured as follows. Section~\ref{sec:background} reviews related work and establishes theoretical foundations. Section~\ref{sec:taxonomy} presents our comprehensive taxonomy of reward hacking behaviors. Section~\ref{sec:methodology} describes our experimental methodology and detection framework. Sections~\ref{sec:study1}, \ref{sec:study2}, and \ref{sec:study3} present our three empirical studies. Section~\ref{sec:results} analyzes results across all studies. Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses implications and limitations, and Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes.
\section{Background and Related Work}
\label{sec:background}

\subsection{Reinforcement Learning and Reward Functions}

Reinforcement Learning agents learn to maximize cumulative reward through environment interaction~\cite{sutton2018reinforcement}. However, designing reward functions that capture complex real-world objectives remains challenging~\cite{ng1999policy}. In domains like autonomous driving, robotics~\cite{kober2013reinforcement}, and game AI~\cite{vinyals2019grandmaster}, practical rewards rely on proxy metrics rather than true objectives, creating opportunities for reward hacking where agents maximize proxies without achieving underlying goals~\cite{kiran2021deep}.

\subsection{Reward Hacking: Definitions and Taxonomy}

Reward hacking, also known as specification gaming~\cite{krakovna2020specification}, occurs when an RL agent exploits flaws, ambiguities, or unintended shortcuts in its reward function to achieve high rewards in ways that are misaligned with the designer's original goals. Skalse et al.~\cite{skalse2022defining} provide the first formal definition, characterizing a proxy reward function as "unhackable" relative to a true reward function if increasing the expected proxy return can never decrease the expected true return.

We identify six primary reward hacking categories: \textbf{Specification Gaming} (satisfying literal reward specification while violating intent~\cite{clark2016faulty}), \textbf{Reward Tampering} (direct interference with reward mechanisms~\cite{everitt2017reward}), \textbf{Proxy Optimization} (optimizing poorly-correlated proxy metrics~\cite{goodhart1984problems}), \textbf{Objective Misalignment} (systematic behavioral deviation~\cite{christian2020alignment}), \textbf{Exploitation Patterns} (exploiting environmental bugs~\cite{murphy2013automatic}), and \textbf{Wireheading} (modifying own reward processing~\cite{orseau2011agents}).

\subsection{Detection and Mitigation Approaches}

Existing detection approaches include manual inspection~\cite{krakovna2020specification}, heuristic-based methods~\cite{clark2016faulty}, and formal verification~\cite{katz2017reluplex}. However, these suffer from scalability issues, high false positive rates, or computational limitations~\cite{dulac2019challenges}. Mitigation approaches include constraint-based learning~\cite{achiam2017constrained,garcia2015comprehensive}, preference learning~\cite{christiano2017deep,brown2019deep}, reward learning from human feedback~\cite{ouyang2022training,stiennon2020learning}, and imitation learning~\cite{ho2016generative}, but require extensive human input or prior knowledge of failure modes.

Recent work explores reward misspecification~\cite{pan2022effects,gao2023scaling}, safe RL~\cite{garcia2015comprehensive,thomas2019preventing}, inverse RL~\cite{abbeel2004apprenticeship,finn2016guided,ho2016generative}, cooperative approaches~\cite{hadfield2016cooperative}, and adversarial reward learning~\cite{fu2017learning}, but lacks comprehensive empirical validation across diverse RL settings.

\subsection{Gaps in Current Research}

Despite growing interest in reward hacking, several critical gaps remain in current research:

\textbf{Limited Empirical Validation}: Most existing work relies on theoretical analysis or small-scale examples, lacking comprehensive empirical studies across diverse RL environments and algorithms.

\textbf{Lack of Automated Detection}: Current detection methods are primarily manual or heuristic-based, limiting their applicability to large-scale RL systems.

\textbf{Insufficient Applied Validation}: Few studies examine reward hacking in realistic application scenarios, limiting understanding of practical prevalence and impact.

\textbf{Limited Mitigation Strategies}: Existing mitigation approaches often require complete reward function redesign, making them impractical for deployed systems.

This paper addresses these gaps through comprehensive empirical analysis, automated detection algorithms, real-world case studies, and practical mitigation techniques.

\section{Reward Hacking Taxonomy and Detection Framework}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

\subsection{Comprehensive Taxonomy}

Building directly on established work in specification gaming~\cite{krakovna2020specification} and reward misalignment~\cite{skalse2022defining}, we operationalize a taxonomy of reward hacking behaviors organized into six primary categories. Our contribution is not the identification of these categories—which have been established in prior work—but rather their systematic operationalization and large-scale empirical validation across diverse RL settings.

Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy} presents our complete taxonomy, including behavioral descriptions, detection indicators, and example manifestations across different RL domains. This taxonomy serves as the foundation for our automated detection framework and guides our empirical analysis.

