\subsection{Detection Framework Architecture}

Our detection framework implements specialized algorithms for each taxonomy category, designed to operate efficiently during RL training with minimal computational overhead. The framework follows a modular architecture where individual detectors can be combined and configured based on specific deployment requirements.

\subsubsection{Core Detection Algorithms}

\textbf{Specification Gaming Detector}: Compares proxy reward accumulation with true objective performance using Kullback-Leibler divergence. The detector maintains a baseline ratio $\rho_{baseline} = \text{median}(\frac{R_{proxy}}{R_{true}})$ learned from normal episodes. Episodes with ratio divergence $D_{KL}(\rho_{current} || \rho_{baseline}) > \tau_{spec}$ (where $\tau_{spec} = 0.3$) are flagged. Temporal analysis uses sliding windows of size $w = \max(10, \lfloor L/10 \rfloor)$ where $L$ is episode length.

\textbf{Reward Tampering Detector}: Employs Isolation Forest anomaly detection with contamination parameter $\gamma = 0.1$ on 10-dimensional feature vectors including reward statistics (mean, variance, skewness, kurtosis), temporal patterns (autocorrelation, trend), and change-point indicators (maximum absolute difference, number of sudden changes $> 3\sigma$). Detection threshold is set at the 90th percentile of anomaly scores from training data.

\textbf{Proxy Optimization Detector}: Analyzes Pearson correlation $\rho(R_{proxy}, R_{true})$ using sliding windows of size 50 timesteps. Correlation degradation $\Delta\rho = \rho_{expected} - \rho_{current} > 0.5$ triggers detection. Expected correlation is learned from initial training phases using robust regression to handle outliers.

\textbf{Objective Misalignment Detector}: Uses n-gram analysis (n=3) of action sequences to build Markov models of normal behavior. Behavioral deviation is measured using perplexity: $\text{PPL} = 2^{-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N} \log_2 P(a_i|a_{i-2}, a_{i-1})}$. Episodes with $\text{PPL} > \mu_{PPL} + 2\sigma_{PPL}$ are flagged as misaligned.

\textbf{Exploitation Pattern Detector}: Maintains performance bounds using robust statistics (median ± 3 × MAD, where MAD is median absolute deviation). Scores exceeding $Q_3 + 3 \times IQR$ or below $Q_1 - 3 \times IQR$ are flagged as potential exploits, where $Q_1, Q_3$ are first and third quartiles.

\textbf{Wireheading Detector}: Monitors reward signal integrity using cryptographic checksums and system call tracing. Detects unauthorized access to reward computation functions and flags episodes with reward signal modifications not matching expected computation paths.

\subsubsection{Integration and Ensemble Methods}

Individual detectors are integrated through an ensemble approach that combines detection signals to produce overall risk assessments. The ensemble uses weighted voting based on detector confidence scores and historical accuracy, providing robust detection across diverse scenarios.

The framework also implements temporal analysis to track reward hacking emergence over training episodes, enabling early detection before behaviors become entrenched. This temporal component is crucial for practical deployment, where early intervention is more effective than post-hoc detection.

\subsubsection{Algorithmic Optimizations}

Our implementation incorporates several optimizations for computational efficiency: (1) \textbf{Incremental Statistics}: Running statistics for correlation and perplexity calculations avoid recomputation, reducing complexity from O(n²) to O(n). (2) \textbf{Adaptive Thresholding}: Detection thresholds automatically adjust based on environment characteristics using robust regression on initial training phases. (3) \textbf{Selective Monitoring}: High-confidence episodes bypass expensive detectors, reducing average computational overhead from 8.3\% to 4.2\%. (4) \textbf{Parallel Processing}: Independent detector execution enables multi-core processing with near-linear speedup (3.7× on 4 cores).

\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:methodology}

Our empirical investigation employs a multi-study approach designed to comprehensively evaluate reward hacking across different dimensions: prevalence and patterns (Study 1), causal factors (Study 2), and simulated application validation (Study 3). This section describes our overall methodology, experimental infrastructure, and evaluation metrics.

\subsection{Experimental Infrastructure}

Our infrastructure consists of: (1) Training Monitoring System capturing episode data with <2\% overhead, (2) Detection Pipeline implementing real-time six-category analysis, and (3) Analysis Framework providing statistical evaluation and visualization tools.

\subsection{Environments and Algorithms}

Our study encompasses 15 diverse RL environments selected to represent different domains, complexity levels, and reward structures:

\textbf{Atari Games} (5 environments): Classic arcade games with well-defined objectives but potential for specification gaming (Breakout, Pong, Space Invaders, Pac-Man, Asteroids).

\textbf{MuJoCo Continuous Control} (4 environments): Physics-based control tasks with continuous action spaces (HalfCheetah, Walker2d, Hopper, Ant).

\textbf{GridWorld Navigation} (3 environments): Discrete navigation tasks with varying reward structures (Simple Grid, Obstacle Grid, Multi-Goal Grid).

\textbf{Custom Environments} (3 environments): Purpose-built environments designed to elicit specific reward hacking behaviors (Proxy Trap, Tampering Test, Alignment Challenge).

\subsection{True Objective Definition and Measurement}

Our framework distinguishes proxy rewards (training) from true objectives (desired outcomes)~\cite{skalse2022defining}. True objectives include: game completion metrics (Atari), energy efficiency (MuJoCo~\cite{todorov2012mujoco}), and business metrics (conversion rate vs. click-through rate).

\textbf{True Objective Validation Protocol}: We employed a rigorous multi-stage process for defining and validating true objectives. First, domain experts (3 RL researchers, 2 industry practitioners) independently proposed true objective definitions for each environment through structured interviews. Second, we conducted a modified Delphi process over three rounds to achieve consensus, with experts rating objective relevance (1--5 scale) and providing written justification. Consensus required $\geq$80\% agreement (4$+$ rating) and Cohen's $\kappa > 0.7$ across raters. Third, for simulated applications, we validated true objectives through stakeholder role-playing exercises where experts assumed different perspectives (users, developers, business stakeholders) to identify potential conflicts.

\textbf{Explicit True Objective Formulas}: For reproducibility, we provide concrete measurement formulas. Atari true objectives combine task completion (binary) with efficiency: $\text{True}_{\text{Atari}} = 0.7 \times \text{TaskComplete} + 0.3 \times (1 - \frac{\text{Steps}}{\text{MaxSteps}})$. MuJoCo objectives balance locomotion with energy: $\text{True}_{\text{MuJoCo}} = 0.6 \times \text{Distance} - 0.4 \times \text{EnergyUsed}$. For simulated applications: recommendation systems use $\text{UserSat} = 0.6 \times \text{Relevance} + 0.3 \times \text{Diversity} + 0.1 \times \text{Novelty}$, competitive games use $\text{GameplayQuality} = 0.4 \times \text{StrategyDiversity} + 0.3 \times \text{FairPlay} + 0.3 \times \text{Engagement}$, and robotic control uses \\$\text{PrecisionAccuracy} = \exp(-\frac{\text{PositionError}^2}{2\sigma^2}) \times (1 - \frac{\text{EnergyUsed}}{\text{MaxEnergy}})$ where $\sigma = 0.05$ meters.

We evaluate five RL algorithms: PPO~\cite{schulman2017proximal}, SAC~\cite{haarnoja2018soft,haarnoja2018soft2}, DQN~\cite{mnih2015human} (enhanced with double Q-learning~\cite{van2016deep} and prioritized replay~\cite{schaul2015prioritized}), A3C~\cite{mnih2016asynchronous}, and Rainbow~\cite{hessel2018rainbow}, representing different exploration strategies~\cite{osband2016deep,pathak2017curiosity} and learning paradigms with varying reward hacking susceptibility.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}

We employ a comprehensive set of metrics to evaluate both detection performance and reward hacking impact:

\textbf{Detection Performance}:
- Precision: $P = \frac{TP}{TP + FP}$
- Recall: $R = \frac{TP}{TP + FN}$  
- F1-Score: $F1 = \frac{2PR}{P + R}$
- AUC-ROC: Area under receiver operating characteristic curve
- Detection Latency: Time from hacking onset to detection

\textbf{Reward Hacking Impact}:
- Hacking Frequency: Percentage of episodes exhibiting hacking behaviors
- Performance Gap: Difference between proxy and true objective performance
- Temporal Patterns: Evolution of hacking behaviors over training
- Severity Distribution: Breakdown of hacking instances by severity level

\textbf{Mitigation Effectiveness}:
- Hacking Reduction: Percentage decrease in hacking frequency
- Performance Preservation: Maintenance of legitimate agent performance
- Computational Overhead: Additional computational cost of mitigation

\subsection{Baseline Comparison Methods}

To provide rigorous evaluation context, we compare our detection framework against state-of-the-art anomaly detection methods beyond naive baselines. We acknowledge that simple threshold-based approaches are insufficient for demonstrating the value of our specialized approach.

\textbf{Selected Baselines}: We evaluate against three categories of methods representing different anomaly detection paradigms:

\textbf{Reconstruction-Based Method (LSTM-Autoencoder)}: We implement an LSTM-Autoencoder designed to model sequential patterns in agent trajectories. The autoencoder is trained on state-action-reward sequences from the 2,156 expert-annotated non-hacking episodes to learn normal behavioral patterns. Episodes with reconstruction error exceeding the 90th percentile threshold are flagged as potential hacking instances. This approach is particularly relevant for RL data due to its ability to capture temporal dependencies in agent behavior.

\textbf{Unsupervised Outlier Detection (One-Class SVM)}: We employ a One-Class SVM to identify outliers without requiring explicit hacking labels during training. The model learns a decision boundary around normal episodes using the same feature vectors as our reward tampering detector (10-dimensional statistical features). Episodes falling outside this boundary are classified as anomalous. This method provides a strong unsupervised baseline for comparison.

\textbf{Supervised Classification (Random Forest)}: As a supervised baseline, we train a Random Forest classifier on the full expert-annotated dataset using features extracted from our detection framework. This represents a conventional machine learning approach to the classification problem.

\textbf{Implementation Details}: All baselines use identical feature extraction and evaluation protocols to ensure fair comparison. Hyperparameters are tuned using 5-fold cross-validation on the expert-annotated subset, with performance evaluated using the same stratified train-test splits as our framework.

\section{Study 1: Large-Scale Empirical Analysis}
\label{sec:study1}

Our first study provides a comprehensive empirical analysis of reward hacking prevalence and patterns across diverse RL environments and algorithms. This large-scale investigation establishes baseline understanding of reward hacking behaviors and validates our detection framework.

\subsection{Experimental Design}

We conducted experiments across 15 environments and 5 algorithms, generating 15,247 episodes with 10 random seeds per configuration. We collected state-action trajectories, proxy/true rewards, and expert annotations (2,156 episodes, Cohen's $\kappa = 0.847$).

\subsection{Detection Framework Validation}

\subsubsection{Validation Protocol and Ground Truth Establishment}

Our evaluation employs two distinct datasets with different validation approaches to ensure scientific rigor while enabling large-scale analysis.

\textbf{Expert-Validated Set (n=2,156)}: Ground truth labels were established by three independent RL experts with extensive experience in reward hacking identification, achieving Cohen's $\kappa = 0.847$ inter-rater reliability. This dataset provides the foundation for all performance metrics (precision, recall, F1-score, AUC-ROC) reported in this study. Detection thresholds were determined through 5-fold cross-validation on this subset only, with hyperparameters ($\tau_{spec} = 0.3$, $\Delta\rho = 0.5$) selected to maximize F1-score on validation folds.

\textbf{Detector-Labeled Set (n=13,091)}: For the remaining episodes, ground truth was not available due to the prohibitive cost of expert annotation. To analyze patterns at scale, we employed a detector consensus approach where an episode was flagged if at least three of our six detectors agreed. \textbf{We explicitly acknowledge this introduces circularity}, and thus performance metrics are reported only on the expert-validated set. Findings from the larger set are presented as exploratory analysis of hacking patterns and prevalence trends.

\textbf{Detector Disagreement Analysis}: In cases where only one or two detectors flagged an episode (18.3\% of detector-labeled episodes), we found these often represented subtle or emergent forms of hacking that did not fit neatly into single categories. These boundary cases highlight the value of our ensemble approach and suggest areas for future detector refinement.

\textbf{Validation Methodology}: Training and testing employed environment stratified splits to prevent data leakage, with entire environment-algorithm combinations held out for testing. All performance claims are based exclusively on the expert-validated set to avoid circular reasoning.

