\subsubsection{Hyperparameter Sensitivity Analysis}

We conducted comprehensive sensitivity analysis on key detection parameters using the expert-annotated subset. Results show moderate robustness: $\tau_{spec}$ variations (0.2-0.4) cause ±0.03 F1-score changes, $\Delta\rho$ variations (0.3-0.7) cause ±0.05 changes, contamination parameter $\gamma$ (0.05-0.15) causes ±0.04 changes, and perplexity threshold multiplier (1.5-2.5) causes ±0.06 changes. The ensemble maintains >75\% F1-score across all tested parameter combinations, indicating reasonable robustness for practical deployment with minimal tuning.

\subsection{Expert-Validated Analysis}

Based on the 2,156 expert-annotated episodes, we observe a hacking frequency of 21.3\%. Atari environments show highest prevalence (28.7\%) due to discrete action spaces~\cite{mnih2015human}, while MuJoCo environments show lowest rates (16.2\%) due to continuous control complexity~\cite{todorov2012mujoco}. A3C exhibits highest hacking rates (26.4\%) due to aggressive exploration~\cite{mnih2016asynchronous}, while SAC shows lowest rates (17.8\%) due to conservative policy updates~\cite{haarnoja2018soft}. 

Category analysis reveals specification gaming dominance (39.8\% of hacking instances), followed by proxy optimization (31.2\%) and objective misalignment (18.7\%). Severity distribution shows most instances are low-medium severity (82\%), with critical cases rare (< 4\%).

\subsection{Exploratory Analysis on Full Dataset}

\textbf{Detector-Labeled Patterns} (15,247 episodes): The larger dataset shows overall frequency of 23.7\% with similar environment and algorithm patterns to the expert-validated subset. Environment-specific trends remain consistent: Atari (31.2\%), MuJoCo (18.4\%), with A3C showing highest susceptibility (28.9\%) and SAC lowest (19.3\%). 

\textbf{Important Caveat}: These broader statistics rely on detector consensus rather than expert ground truth and may be optimistically biased due to circular labeling. They are presented for exploratory pattern analysis only, not as validated performance claims.

\subsection{Temporal Patterns}

Hacking emerges mid-training (episodes 200-800) as agents develop sufficient capability to discover exploits~\cite{clark2016faulty}. Three patterns: Gradual Emergence (67\%), Sudden Onset (24\%), Intermittent (9\%). Figure~\ref{fig:temporal_patterns} shows detection points for each pattern type.

\textbf{Algorithm-Pattern Correlations}: We observe significant correlations between RL algorithms and temporal patterns. A3C's aggressive exploration leads to more Sudden Onset patterns (34\% vs. 24\% average), as rapid policy changes can quickly discover exploits. SAC shows predominantly Gradual Emergence patterns (78\% vs. 67\% average) due to its conservative policy updates. PPO exhibits the most Intermittent patterns (14\% vs. 9\% average), likely due to its clipped objective causing periodic exploitation discovery. These correlations suggest that algorithm choice influences not just hacking frequency but also the temporal dynamics of emergence.

