1. Prioritize alignment with true objectives over simplicity
2. Use dense reward signals when possible to guide learning
3. Carefully evaluate complex reward functions for exploitation opportunities
4. Consider interaction effects when combining design choices

\section{Study 3: Simulated Application Studies}
\label{sec:study3}

Our third study validates our detection framework through three simulated application scenarios, demonstrating applicability across diverse domains.

We designed three simulated environments: (1) Recommendation System with click-through vs. satisfaction metrics, (2) Competitive Game with win rate vs. gameplay quality, and (3) Robotic Control with speed vs. precision trade-offs.

\textbf{Key Results}: Hacking occurred in 11.6-18.7\% of episodes across domains. The recommendation system showed 34\% proxy-true reward gaps, competitive games achieved 89\% win rates with only 23\% gameplay quality, and robotic control gained 78\% speed while losing 43\% precision. Mitigation techniques reduced hacking frequency to 2.9-4.2\% across all domains while maintaining 82-97\% of original performance.

