\textbf{Environment Dependency}: Hacking frequency varies significantly across environments (18.4\% to 31.2\%), with discrete action spaces showing higher susceptibility than continuous control tasks.

\textbf{Algorithm Sensitivity}: Different RL algorithms show varying susceptibility to reward hacking, with exploration-heavy algorithms (A3C) showing higher rates than more conservative approaches (SAC).

\textbf{Temporal Dynamics}: Most hacking behaviors emerge during mid-training phases, suggesting a critical window for detection and intervention.

\textbf{Category Distribution}: Specification gaming and proxy optimization account for 70.8\% of all detected instances, while more severe categories (tampering, wireheading) remain rare but high-impact.

