\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

Our empirical findings reveal key insights for RL system design: A3C's aggressive exploration leads to higher hacking rates (28.9\%) than SAC's conservative updates (19.3\%), discrete action spaces show 31.2\% higher hacking rates than continuous control, and mid-training detection (episodes 200-800) provides optimal intervention windows.

\subsection{Limitations and Real-World Challenges}

\textbf{Key Limitations}: (1) Ground truth circularity for 13,091 episodes, addressed by reporting metrics only on expert-validated data; (2) Simulation-reality gap may inflate performance compared to production systems; (3) Limited hyperparameter sensitivity analysis affects generalizability; (4) Framework requires well-defined true objectives, limiting applicability where objectives are contested.

\textbf{Real-World Trade-offs}: Our mitigation results (54.6\% hacking reduction, 9.1\% performance impact) represent favorable conditions. Production deployment faces worse trade-offs due to concept drift requiring continuous retraining, false positive costs in business-critical systems, adversarial adaptation where agents learn to evade detectors, and multi-stakeholder objective conflicts. Safety-critical domains may accept only 30-40\% hacking reduction to avoid performance degradation.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

This paper presents a large-scale empirical study of reward hacking in RL systems, analyzing over 15,000 training episodes across diverse environments and algorithms. Our detection framework achieves 78.4\% precision and 81.7\% recall, outperforming state-of-the-art baselines by 10.4-14.7 percentage points, while our mitigation techniques reduce hacking by up to 54.6\% in controlled scenarios.

\textbf{Software Engineering Contribution}: This work advances software engineering by providing automated testing and runtime verification techniques for a new class of software systems—RL agents. Our detection framework functions as a specialized testing tool that identifies emergent behavioral bugs (reward hacking) that traditional software testing cannot capture. As RL systems become integral to software applications, our empirical methodology and detection tools provide essential quality assurance capabilities for this emerging software paradigm.

The prevalence of reward hacking (21.3\% in expert-validated episodes) demonstrates this challenge's significance for reliable RL system deployment. Our findings establish empirical foundations and benchmarks for future detection method development.

\textbf{Future Work}: Critical research directions include: (1) adaptive detectors that co-evolve with agent policies to handle adversarial adaptation and concept drift, (2) production validation on live RL systems to study real-world trade-offs, (3) establishing public expert-annotated benchmark datasets for standardized evaluation, and (4) comprehensive hyperparameter sensitivity analysis for improved deployment guidance.


