From diffeomorphism groups,
we will later be able to interpret differentiable maps between Euclidean spaces, and hence smooth manifolds embedded in Euclidean spaces. This observation, combined with Theorem~\ref{thm:mfd} and Proposition~\ref{prop:homeomorphic}, implies the following.

\begin{cor}\label{cor:exotic}
For a given closed, smooth manifold $M$
there exists a sentence $\epsilon_M$ such that
if $G\models \epsilon_M$ 
for some  group $G$ satisfying
\[\Diff^\infty_{0(,\omega)}(M)\leq G\leq \Diff^1_{(\omega)}(M),\]
then there is a manifold that is homeomorphic to $M$ but not diffeomorphic to $M$.
\end{cor}

As an application of Corollary~\ref{cor:exotic}, there is a sentence which asserts ``I am
an exotic $4$--sphere". Verifying the validity of the sentence $\epsilon_M$ in a
diffeomorphism group appears practically impossible; see~\cite{KdlN23}.


