\section{Preliminaries and the homeomorphism type of manifolds}
We now gather some generalities that we will require in the sequel.

\subsection{First order theory of diffeomorphism groups and consequences in the
continuous category}\label{ss:continuous}
The basic reference for this section is~\cite{kkdln2025}, and we will be using
notation from that paper.
For an arbitrary closed smooth manifold, we will show that
the first order theory of an arbitrary group
$G$ satisfying
\[\Diff^\infty_{0(,\omega)}(M)\leq G\leq \Diff^1_{(\omega)}(M)\] is at least as powerful
as the first order theory of the homeomorphism group of $M$. In particular, we will
obtain that smooth manifolds with elementarily equivalent diffeomorphism groups
(of arbitrary regularity) are automatically homeomorphic.

The goal of this section is to indicate how the methods in~\cite{kkdln2025} can be
adapted to conservatively expand the language of groups to interpret various new
sorts in the diffeomorphism group of a compact topological manifold $M$, possibly with boundary; in~\cite{kkdln2025}, this expansion
was called AGAPE, and included the following parameter-free definable
sorts and associated predicates:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The regular open sets $\ro(M)$ of $M$, together with a predicate expressing
    the natural $G$-action on regular open sets.
    \item Full second order arithmetic \[(\N,+,\times,<,0,\subseteq),\] together with
    predicates identifying subsets of natural numbers with components of regular open
    sets.
    \item The sort of finite tuples of points $\mathcal P^{<\infty}(M)$, together with
    predicates expressing membership of a point in a regular open set, as well as
    a definable exponential function \[\exp\colon G\times\Z\times M\longrightarrow M\]
    satisfying $\exp(g,n,p)=q$ if and only if $g^n(p)=q$.
    \item The sort of parametrized regular open balls in $M$.
    \item A predicate expressing that a regular open set is equal to a tubular neighborhood of the boundary of $M$.
\end{itemize}

Throughout this section, $M$ will denote an arbitrary closed smooth
manifold. Recall our convention that whenever $G$ preserves
a volume form on $M$, we will assume that $M$ has dimension at least
two.

\subsubsection{Transitivity of balls}
We will utilize balls in a smooth $n$--manifold $M$ for our proof.
Let us write $B(k)$ for the open ball of radius $k$ centered at the origin in $\bR^n$. An \emph{(smooth) embedded collared ball}, or simply a \emph{collared ball} is given by a $C^\infty$--embedding \[\phi\colon B(1)\longrightarrow M,\]
where $B(1)$ is a ball of radius one in $\bR^n$, such that $\phi$ extends to a $C^\infty$--diffeomorphic embedding of $B(2)$. We call $\phi(B(2))\setminus \phi(B(1))$ the
\emph{collar} of the ball. We will say two collared balls
\[\phi_1,\phi_2\colon B(1)\longrightarrow M\] have disjoint collars if there exist extensions of $\phi_1$ and $\phi_2$ to $B(2)$ with disjoint images.

If $n=1$ then collared balls are identical with intervals whose closures not equal to the entirety of $M$. In this case, if $\{B_1,\ldots,B_k\}$ are embedded collared balls in $M$ with disjoint collars, we say that $B_i$ and $B_{i+1}$ are \emph{adjacent} (in the natural linear or cyclic order on $M$) if there is a path from $B_i$ to $B_{i+1}$ which does not meet the interior of $B_j$ for any $j\neq i,i+1$. We will say that $\{B_1,\ldots,B_k\}$ is \emph{ordered} if $B_i$ is adjacent to $B_{i+1}$ for $1\leq i\leq k-1$. There is an obvious notion of an \emph{order preserving} bijection between collections of balls $\{B_1,\ldots,B_k\}$
and $\{B_1',\ldots,B_k'\}$, and we say these collections are \emph{order isomorphic} if such a bijection exists. It is trivial to see that in dimension one, for $k$ arbitrary and two collections $\{B_1,\ldots,B_k\}$ and $\{B_1',\ldots,B_k'\}$ of collared balls with disjoint collars, there exists a smooth diffeomorphism $\phi$ with $B_i\mapsto B_i'$ for all $i$ if and only
if there is an order preserving bijection between $\{B_1,\ldots,B_k\}$
and $\{B_1',\ldots,B_k'\}$.


\begin{lemma}\label{lem:cr-trans-balls-gen}
Let $M$ be a smooth connected manifold.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item If $\dim M=1$, then $\Diff_0^\infty(M)$ acts transitively on order-isomorphic ordered
    $k$--tuples of collared balls with disjoint collars.
    \item\label{p:transitive}  If $\dim M\geq 2$, 
    and if $(U_1,\ldots,U_k)$ and $(V_1,\ldots,V_k)$ are two $k$--tuples of collared balls with disjoint collars 
    in $M$,
    then there exists some $g\in \Diff_0^\infty(M)$ such that 
    $g(U_i)= V_i$ for all $i$.
    \item 
    The group $\Diff_0^\infty(M)$ acts transitively on collared balls of the same volume.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}
%The property in (\ref{p:transitive-inclusive}) for $k=1$ is called \emph{transitive-inclusivity}; see~\cite{Ling1984} and~\cite[Definition 3.3.6]{KK2021book}.

\begin{proof}
We have argued the case where $\dim M=1$ already. In higher dimension, observe first
that after applying a $C^{\infty}$ diffeomorphism of $M$,
we may reduce to the case where $\{B_1,\ldots,B_k\}$
and $\{B_1',\ldots,B_k'\}$ lie in a single smooth chart of $M$. The lemma then follows
from 
%uniform approximations of smooth maps by $C^\infty$ maps and from 
a straightforward application of smooth isotopy extension theorem; see~\cite[Chapter 8]{hirsch2012differential}).
    The proof of the last part is identical to
    Corollary 2.2 in~\cite{LeRoux2014}, replacing the
    Oxtoby--Ulam Theorem by Moser's Theorem and using the previous parts
    for transitivity on smooth balls; cf.~\cite{kkdln2025}.
\end{proof}


The $C^0$--version of the following is Lemma 2.5 in~\cite{kkdln2025}. The proof for
smooth manifolds is more intuitive.


\begin{lemma}\label{lem:trans-measure}
If $U\subseteq M$ is a connected open set of volume $A>0$, then for all $0<a<A$ there exists a (smooth) collared ball $B$ of volume $a$, embedded in $U$. Moreover, if $\dim M
\geq 2$, then we may
arrange for $U\setminus B$ to be connected.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Fix an $\epsilon>0$ which is less than $\min\{A-a,a\}$.
The subset $U$ is a smooth submanifold of $M$, which is therefore triangulable.
By taking finitely many simplices in a triangulation, we may find a
finite triangulation $T$
of
%\comment{subset $K$ to bounded smooth submanifold $K$? Are we using (the deep) smooth approximation theorem?}
a compact subset $K$ of $U$, whose volume is at least $a+\epsilon$. The top-dimensional
cells in $T$ can be shrunk down to (pairwise disjoint) smooth balls, so that their total union $V$ has
volume contained in $[a-\epsilon,a)$. Now, choose finitely many disjoint smooth paths
$\{\gamma_i\}_{i\in I}$ between 
components of
$V$, to obtain a connected superset $X$ of $V$. A graph $\gam$ can be built by taking
the components of $V$ as vertices and the edge relation being connectedness by a path
in $\{\gamma_i\}_{i\in I}$. Choose a maximal tree in $\gam$ and delete the paths whose
indices correspond to edges outside the maximal tree. Thickening the remaining paths
slightly, we get a submanifold $B$ of $U$ which is clearly diffeomorphic to a
smooth collared ball. Since the choice of $\epsilon$ was arbitrary, it is easy to see
that we may arrange for $W$ to have volume exactly $a$.

In dimension at least two, no ball that is compactly contained in the interior of
$U$ will separate $U$. The ball $B$ is itself diffeomorphic to a smooth ball, so
$U\setminus B$ is in fact connected.
\end{proof}

