\begin{lem}\cite{CK1970}\label{lem:c-infty-ck}
For each $\underline{f}\in\EE^{\infty}(n,k)$ and for each $\epsilon>0$,  there exists $\delta>0$ such that every $\underline{f}\in\EE^{\infty}(n,k)$ that is at most $\delta$--far from $\underline f$ by the $C^{\infty}$--distance admits a $C^{\infty}$ diffeomoprhism 
\[ C(\underline{f})\longrightarrow  C(\underline{f'})\] 
that is at most $\epsilon$--far from the identity map by the $C^{\infty}$--distance. \end{lem}
\begin{proof}
	We fix $\underline{f}$ as in the statement of the lemma,
    and consider $\underline{f}'\in\EE^{\infty}(n,k)$ arbitrary. We write 
	$D=d(\underline{f},\underline{f}')$ for the $C^{\infty}$--distance.
    For all \[1\leq j\leq k\quad\textrm{and} \quad 1\leq \ell\leq k+1,\] we write
	 \begin{equation*}
