\section{The Main Theorem and its consequences}\label{s:diffeo}
The main result of this paper is now straightforward.
\begin{proof}[Proof of the Main Theorem, for dimension at least two]
Under the hypotheses of the theorem, we first deduce that $M\cong N$ from Theorem~\ref{thm:mfd}. Assuming $r<s$, we then pick $q\in\bQ$ such that $1\le r\le q<s\le\infty$. We have that $G\models \phi_q$ by part (\ref{p:reg1}) of Theorem~\ref{thm:reg}. This violates part (\ref{p:reg2}) of the same theorem, since we have assumed $H\le\Diff^s(N)$.
\end{proof}

We finish this paper with an argument in dimension one.
The basic strategy is to mimic an idea in~\cite{KK2020crit} that constructs homeomorphisms
of the interval and of the circle of specified regularity. The following result is an
immediate consequence of Theorem 2.9 in~\cite{KK2020crit}:

\begin{lem}\label{lem:delta-fast-1}
   Let $r\in\R_{\geq 1}$ be written as $r=k+\tau$, where $k\in\N$ and $\tau\in [0,1)$, and let
   $\delta>0$.
   Suppose that:
   \begin{enumerate}
       \item $f$ is a $C^r$ diffeomorphism of $I$.
       \item $f$ is supported on an infinite collection
       $\{J_i\subseteq I\}_{i\in\omega}$ of disjoint intervals
       such that $\sup J_i<\inf J_{i+1}$ for each $i$.
       \item $\{N_i\}_{i\in\omega}\subseteq \N$ is a sequence of natural numbers such that
       \[\sup_i \frac{N_i}{i^{k-1+\tau}}<\infty.\]
   \end{enumerate}
   Write $i\in A_f$ if there exists a point $x\in J_i$ such that
   \[\frac{|f^{N_i}(x)-x|}{|J_i|}\geq \delta.\] Then
   \[\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{A_f\cap [0,n]}{n}= 0.\]
\end{lem}

The following follows immediately from Theorem 2.14 in~\cite{KK2020crit}:
\begin{lem}\label{lem:delta-fast-2}
    Let $r\in\R_{\geq 1}$ be written as $r=k+\tau$, where $k\in\N$ and $\tau\in [0,1)$,
    let $\delta>0$, and let $\{J_i\subseteq I\}_{i\in\omega}$ be a collection
    of disjoint intervals
       such that $\sup J_i<\inf J_{i+1}$ for each $i$. If $\{N_i\}_{i\in\omega}\subseteq \N$
       is a sequence of natural numbers such that \[\inf_i N_i\cdot |J_i|^{k-1+\tau}\geq 1,\]
       then there exists a $C^r$ diffeomorphism $f$ of $I$ such that:
       \begin{enumerate}
           \item $f$ is supported on $\bigcup_i J_i$.
           \item For all $i$, there exists a point $x\in J_i$ such that
           \[\frac{|f^{N_i}(x)-x|}{|J_i|}\geq \delta.\]
       \end{enumerate}
\end{lem}

\begin{proof}[Proof of the Main Theorem, in dimension one]
    Here we suppose that $\dim M=1$; since $M$ is closed and connected it must be
    homeomorphic to the circle, and so the groups of length-preserving diffeomorphisms
    of $M$ are just the groups of Euclidean rotations. We therefore only consider general
    (i.e.~not volume preserving)
    groups of diffeomorphisms.

    Since $N$ is homeomorphic to $M$, we have that $N$ must be the circle as well; it
    is immediate that $M$ and $N$ are diffeomorphic to each other. To detect
    the regularity of the diffeomorphism group, we start with finding a 
    definable smooth
    parametrized interval in $S^1$; this can be obtained from the Bochner--Montgomery
    Theorem and the same argument as in Proposition~\ref{prop:smooth-ball}. We fix
    such an interval \[\phi\colon I\longrightarrow S^1.\] If we equip $S^1$ with the
    usual angular metric and $I$ with the usual Euclidean metric, it follows from the fact
    that $\phi$ is smooth that the induced angular metric on $\phi(I)\subseteq S^1$
    is bi-Lipschitz equivalent to the Euclidean metric on $I$.

    We now use Lemma~\ref{lem:delta-fast-1} and Lemma~\ref{lem:delta-fast-2} to show that
    if $G^r\equiv G^s$ then $r=s$. Indeed, if $r<s$ then we choose a rational $\alpha$
    between $r$ and $s$ and find a formula that is satisfied by an element of $G^r$ but
    by no element of $G^s$, which expresses that such an element is a $C^{\alpha}$
    diffeomorphism of the circle but is not smoother. We encode a sequence of disjoint
    intervals $\{J_i\subseteq I\}_{i\in\omega}$ with rational endpoints and
    satisfying $\sup J_i<\inf J_{i+1}$ for all $i$, by a single definable real number.
    We can then construct a formula $\rho_{\alpha}$ as in Theorem~\ref{thm:reg}
    (though the details are simpler in this case)
    which is satisfied by some element of $G^r$ but no element of $G^s$. Switching the
    roles of $r$ and $s$ gives us the desired conclusion.
\end{proof}

