[0,1)$.
We choose a sequence $\{J_i\subseteq I\}_{i\in\omega}$ of disjoint intervals with rational
endpoints, such that $\sup J_i<\inf J_{i+1}$ for each $i$, and write $\ell_i=|J_i|$. We
define a sequence of boxes $\{B_i\subseteq I^{n-1}\}_{i\in\omega}$ by \[B_i=J_i\times
[1/2-\ell_i,1/2+\ell_i]^{n-2}\] for each $i$. Note that if the intervals
$\{J_i\}_{i\in\omega}$ are chosen judiciously then they (as well as the boxes $\{B_i\}_{i\in
\omega}$) can be encoded as a single definable real number $\theta$.

\begin{lem}\label{lem:delta-fast-rotation}
    Let \[I^{n-1}\times S^1=T\subseteq M\] be a fixed smooth submanifold.
    Let $0\leq \delta<1/2$ be rational and let $\{N_i\}_{i\in\omega}\subseteq \N$
       be a sequence of natural numbers such that
       \[\inf_i N_i\cdot \ell_i^{k-1+\tau}\geq 1.\] Then there exists a $C^{\alpha}$ function
       \[f\colon I^{n-1}\longrightarrow S^1\] supported on $\bigcup_i B_i$ such that:
       \begin{enumerate}
           \item The map $\Phi_f$ extends by the identity to a $C^{\alpha}$ diffeomorphism
           of $M$.
           \item For all $i\in\omega$, there is a point $x\in B_i$
           such that $\psi_{x,f}^{N_i}$ is a rotation by at least $\delta$.
       \end{enumerate}
       Moreover, if $G$ consists of $C^{\alpha}$ diffeomorphisms of $M$ then the set of
       diffeomorphisms $g\in G$ satisfying these conditions is definable, with $T$ and
       $\theta$ as parameters.
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
It will be convenient for us to consider a smooth parametrization of the form $T:=I\times[-1,1]^{n-2}\times S^1$.
From Lemma~\ref{lem:delta-fast-2}, we have a $C^\alpha$ map
\[
g\co I\to [0,1]\]
supported in $\coprod_i J_i$ such that 
the supremum of $g$ on $J_i$ is at least $\delta/N_i$. 
Let us fix a smooth map 
\[\lambda\co\bR^{n-2}\to[0,1]\]
supported in $[-1/2,1/2]^{n-2}$ such that $\lambda(O)=1$.
For each $x\in I$ and $y\in[-1,1]^{n-2}$, we then define 
\[
f(x,y):=g(x)\sum_i \delta_{J_i}(x)\lambda(y/\ell_i).\]
In other words, we consider the self map $\Phi$ of $I\times[-1,1]^{n-2}\times S^1$ defined by
\[
\Phi(x,y,\theta)=(x,y,\theta+f(x,y)).\]
Let us consider partial sums
\[
g_N(x):=\sum_{i=1}^N g(x) \delta_{J_i}(x),
\quad f_N(x,y):=g(x)\sum_{i=1}^N \delta_{J_i}(x)\lambda(y/\ell_i).\]
For each $x\in J_i$, we have
\[
D^j g(x) =
\underbrace{\int\int\cdots\int}_{k-j\text{ times}} D^kg\]
for suitable limits in $\cl J_i$. This implies that
\[
[D^k f_N]_{\tau}\le M [D^k g_N]_\tau\]
for some $M>0$ depending on $\lambda$ and $\{\ell_i\}$.
A typical Arzela--Ascoli argument proves that $f$ is a $C^\alpha$ map. Since the Jacobian of $\Phi$ is $1$, we obtain that $\Phi$ is a $C^\alpha$ diffeomorhism. The rest of the conclusion is clear.
\end{proof}

We retain the regularity modulus $\alpha=k+\tau$.
Now, let $I^{n-1}\times S^1=T\subseteq M$ be a fixed smoothly parametrized submanifold,
and identify $I^{n-1}$ with $I^{n-1}\times\{0\}$ in this parametrization. Fix
intervals $\{J_i\subseteq I\}_{i\in\omega}$ and the boxes
$\{B_i\subseteq I^{n-1}\}_{i\in\omega}$ as above, and write $\ell_i=|J_i|$; encode the
data of the boxes $\{B_i\}_{i\in\omega}$ by a real number $\theta$. Consider a formula
$\phi_{\alpha}(\gamma,T,\theta)$, which expresses the following for an element $g\in G$:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $g$ is the identity outside of \[\bigcup_i B_i\times S^1\] and agrees on $T$ with
    $\Phi_f$ for some circle valued continuous function $f$ defined on $I^{n-1}$.
    \item For all $i$ there exists a point $x\in B_i$ such that $\psi_{x,f}^{N_i}$ has order
    exactly three, where here $N_i$ is the smallest integer greater than
    $1/\ell_i^{k-1+\tau}$.
\end{enumerate}

It is straightforward to see that the first order formula $\phi_{\alpha}$ exists.

\begin{lem}\label{lem:beta-alpha}
    Let $\beta\geq 1$ be real. If $\beta>\alpha$ then no $C^{\beta}$ diffeomorphism
    $g$ satisfies $\phi_{\alpha}(g,T,\theta)$.
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
    This should use the fact that diffeomorphisms are bi-Lipschitz and displacement estimates
    from derivative bounds.
\end{proof}
\fi


