\section{The minimal free resolution of the residue field}

In this paper, let $(R,\fm, \kk)$ be a local ring with maximal ideal $\fm$ and residue field $\kk=R/\fm$. Let $n$ be the embedding dimension of $R$ and $c$ its codepth. Let $(K,\partial)$ be the Koszul complex on the minimal set of generators of $\fm$ and $A=A_0\oplus A_1\oplus \cdots \oplus A_c$ its Koszul homology. As in \cite{NV, NV2}, we set for all $0\les i\les c$
\[a_i\coloneqq \rank_{\kk} (A_i) \qquad \text{and} \qquad 
\{[z^i_1],\dots,[z^i_{a_i}]\} 
\text{ be a $\kk$-basis for $A_i$},\] 
where $z^i_u$ is a cycle representative in $K_i$. Here, the superscript in $z^i_u$ denotes the homological degree and the subscript in $z^i_u$ denotes an index.

The main result of this section is \Cref{graded res}. It provides a way to construct a minimal free resolution of the residue field $\kk$ over the local ring $R$ starting from a graded minimal free resolution of $\kk$ as a module over the graded $\kk$-algebra $A$. We first set the following convention to be used throughout the paper. 

\begin{convention}
We denote the following maps by 
\[
\alpha \oplus \beta = \left(\begin{array}{c|c}\alpha&0\\ \hline 0&\beta \end{array}\right) \colon 
\xymatrixcolsep{9pt}
\xymatrixrowsep{6pt}
\xymatrix{
M \ar@{->}[r]^{\alpha} \ar@{}[d]|{\oplus}&N\ar@{}[d]|{\oplus}\\
M' \ar@{->}[r]^{\beta} &N'
}
\qquad \qquad
\begin{pmatrix}\alpha\\ \hline \beta\end{pmatrix} \colon 
\xymatrix{
M \ar@{->}[r]^{\alpha} \ar@{->}[rd]_{\beta} &N \ar@{}[d]|{\oplus} \\ &N'
} 
\qquad \qquad \text{ and } \qquad
(\alpha|\beta) \colon
\xymatrix{
M \ar@{->}[r]^{\alpha} \ar@{}[d]|{\oplus} & N. \\ M' \ar@{->}[ru]_{\beta} & 
}
\]
Likewise, for any integer $k\ges 1$ we denote by $\alpha^{k}\coloneqq \alpha^{k-1}\oplus \alpha: M^{\oplus k} \to N^{\oplus k}$ with $\alpha: M \to N$ appearing on the main diagonal $k$ times, and $0$ everywhere else. 

For any two complexes $(L,\partial^{L})$ and $(L',\partial^{L'})$ of modules over a ring, we denote by $(L\oplus L', \partial^{L}\oplus \partial^{L'})$ the direct sum of complexes. In particular, for any integer $k\ges 1$ we set $L^{k} \coloneqq L \oplus L \oplus \cdots \oplus L$ for a direct sum of $k$ copies of $L$. For any chain map $\lambda: L \to L'$, we denote $\lambda^k: L^k \to (L')^k$ for a direct sum of $k$ copies of $\lambda$ with respective components. 
We use the subscript to denote the homological component of $L$, and superscript with parentheses to label different complexes.

For a complex $L$ where $L_i=0$ for all $i <0$, we use the convention in \cite{BH} and say $L$ is \emph{exact} if $\HH{i}{L}=0$ for all $i \ges 0$; and $L$ is \emph{acyclic} if $\HH{i}{L}=0$ for all $i > 0$.



Let $L$ be a graded complex over a graded ring $S$ with $L_i = \bigoplus_{j \in \BZ} S^{b_{i,j}}(-j)$ for all $i$ and some non-negative integers $b_{i,j}$. Then, for $a\in \BZ$, $L(-a)$ denotes the graded complex with $L(-a)_i=\bigoplus_{s \in \BZ} S^{b_{i,j}}(-j-a)$.
\end{convention}


\medskip
The following is our main result, which shows that resolving $\kk$ over any local ring $R$ could amount to resolving $\kk$ over the Koszul homology $A$. 

\begin{theorem}
\label{graded res}
Let $(R,\fm,\kk)$ be a local ring of codepth $c \ges 1$, $K$ the Koszul complex of $R$ on a minimal set of generators of $\fm$, and $A$ its Koszul homology. Set complexes $\sC^{(0)} \coloneqq K$, $\sC^{(k)} \coloneqq \bigoplus_{s\ges 0} \Sigma^s K^{u_{k,s}}$ for all integers $k \ges 1$ and some non-negative integers $u_{k,s}$. Let $\{\varphi^{(k)} \colon \sC^{(k)} \to 
    \sC^{(k-1)}\}_{k \ges 1}$ be a sequence of chain maps. If the induced complex   
\begin{equation*}
    \BF\colon \dots\to \bigoplus_{s\ges 0} A^{u_{k,s}}(-s)\xra{\partial_k^\BF} \bigoplus_{s\ges 0} A^{u_{k-1,s}}(-s)\xra{\partial_{k-1}^\BF}\dots \to \bigoplus_{s\ges 0} A^{u_{1,s}}(-s) \xra{\partial_{1}^\BF} A
\end{equation*}
with differentials $\partial_k^\BF=[\varphi^{(k)}]$, is a graded minimal resolution of $\kk$ as an $A$-module, then the limit of the iterated mapping cone of the sequence of maps $\{\varphi^{(k)}\}_{k \ges 1}$ is a minimal free resolution of $\kk$ as an $R$-module.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof} 
Consider the notation from \cite[Section 3]{NV2}. Let $\{\sM^{(j)},f^{(j)}\}_{j \ges 0}$ denote the iterated mapping cone sequence of $\{\varphi^{(j)}\}_{j \ges 1}$ and let $F$ denote the limit mapping cone, as in \cite[Construction 3.1 and Definition 3.2]{NV2}. By construction, $F$ is a complex, that is,  $\im(\partial_k^F) \subseteq \Ker(\partial_{k-1}^F)$ for all $k \ges 1$.

To show exactness of $F$ at each $k \ges 1$, that is, the equality $\im(\partial_k^F)=\Ker(\partial_{k-1}^F)$ holds for all $k \ges 1$, we use induction on $k$ and an argument similar to the proof of \cite[Theorem 4.1]{NV2}. Remark that for $i=\left\lceil\frac{k}{2}\right\rceil$, we have $F_{k+1} = \sM^{(i+1)}_{k+1}$, $F_{k} = \sM^{(i+1)}_{k}$, and $F_{k-1} = \sM^{(i+1)}_{k-1}$. Therefore, the problem reduces to showing the exactness of the sequence $\sC^{(i+1)}_{k+1}\to\sC^{(i+1)}_{k}\to\sC^{(i+1)}_{k-1}$ for each $k \ges 1$. 
For $k=2j$ and $k=2j+1$, this translates to showing that we have the following equalities of homologies:
\begin{equation*}
    \HH{2j}{\sC^{(j+1)}}=0=\HH{2j+1}{\sC^{(j+1)}},\quad\text{ for all $j\ges 1$}.
\end{equation*}  

We now follow the ideas in the proofs of \cite[Lemma 3.6 and Theorem 3.7]{NV2}. The iterated mapping cone construction yields the following diagram with exact rows
\begin{equation*}
\label{cone diagram c}
\xymatrixrowsep{2pc}
 \xymatrixcolsep{3pc}
\xymatrix{
0\ar@{->}[r]&
\Sigma^{-1}\sC^{(j)}\ar@{->}[r]^{\Sigma^{-1}\sff^{(j)}}&
\Sigma^{-1}\sC^{(j+1)}\ar@{->}[r]^{\Sigma^{-1}\sg^{(j+1)}}&
\Sigma^{j} \sC^{(j+1)}\ar@{->}[d]^{\Sigma^{j}\varphi^{(j+1)}}\ar@{.>}[dl]_{\psi^{(j+1)}}\ar@{->}[r]&0\\
0\ar@{->}[r]&\sC^{(j-1)}\ar@{->}[r]^{\sff^{(j-1)}}&\sC^{(j)}\ar@{->}[r]^{\sg^{(j)}}&\Sigma^{j} \sC^{(j)}\ar@{->}[r]&0
}
\end{equation*}
which induce the rows of long exact sequences of homology that fit in \Cref{fig c}. 

From the definition of $\sC^{(j)}$, the hypotheses $[\varphi^{(j)}]=\partial_j^\BF$ in the Koszul homology $A$ for all $j\ges 1$, and the acyclicity of $\BF$, we obtain that for each $v\ges 0$, there exists an exact sequence of homologies:
\begin{equation*}
    \dots\to \HH{v}{\sC^{(j)}}\xra{\HH{v}{\varphi^{(j)}}} \HH{v}{\sC^{(j-1)}}\xra{\HH{v}{\varphi^{(j-1)}}} \dots \to \HH{v}{\sC^{(1)}}\xra{\HH{v}{\varphi^{(1)}}} \HH{v}{\sC^{(0)}}\to 0. 
\end{equation*}
Therefore, the vertical sequences in \Cref{fig c} are exact. 
This is all that is needed in the proof by induction of \cite[Lemma 3.6]{NV2}. As in the proof of \cite[Theorem 3.7]{NV2} we conclude that    
\begin{equation*}
\label{eq H}
\HH{k}{\sC^{(j+1)}} \cong \begin{cases}
\HH{k}{\sC^{(j)}}, & \quad 0\les k \les 2j \text{ or } k \ges 2j+c+3 \\
0 & \quad k=2j+1 \text{ or } k=2j+2
\end{cases}
\quad\text{for all }  k,j\ges 0,
\end{equation*}
which yields the desired equalities $\HH{2j}{\sM^{(j+1)}}\cong \HH{2j}{\sM^{(j)}}=0$ and $\HH{2j+1}{\sM^{(j+1)}}=0$, for all $j\ges 1$. Hence, $F$ is exact.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
All superscripts in \Cref{fig c} should have parentheses around them to label different complexes or maps, we omit writing the parentheses there for simplicity of notation. 
\end{remark}


